chapter-14.md 100 It was the outer wrapper of a seat-courier's packet, heavy grey paper stamped across in red, *BY SEAT COURIER. BY HAND.* He looked at it for some time. Then he looked at the boy's folder lying shut on his blotter, and opened it, and found on top of his own nine leaves a single sheet of registry-seat stock with a seal pressed into it.
chapter-14.md 118 *Minute of the 40th: evaluation seat, to be called. Refusal of the 44th: fully constituted. Who sat down in those five chairs in four days, without asking this office for the file?*
chapter-15.md 113 "No. Think who he is and who you are. He's the Compact's senior evaluator, and he's just asked to put a finding into proceedings where this house is the respondent. A letter from this house on his desk this week, about anything at all, would be on the file by morning; and his finding, if it's ever given, would be read for the rest of its life as something the respondent asked him for." She shook her head. "The kindest thing this house can do for that man is leave him alone."
chapter-16.md 19 The real errand had been the board, but he had a docket number to ask Bracken about, so he went back by the records hall. The counter's daybook lay open on the brass rail where Bracken had left it to dry, and Cael read the day's line before he could stop himself. It was a page of figures and he had never in his life been able to leave a page of figures unread. Four narrow columns had been ruled across it in the last few weeks, in Bracken's small capitals: *HOUSES. SPONSORS. FACULTIES. OTHER.* Under *OTHER*, yesterday, the figure was one hundred and forty-one. The heaviest line on the page, the Fifth-day before the sitting, said one hundred and ninety-six.
chapter-16.md 161 Under his own name there was nothing to write. He drew a short line at the foot of each of the four columns, the way Karis had told him to, so that each could be added up; and under the first three he wrote a figure, the number of lines above it. Under his own he wrote *0*, and looked at it for a long time, and did not write anything beside it.
chapter-17.md 121 He was on one knee on the bottom step, threading a chain through the stair's rail. It was a long chain, the kind the porters used on the cart-shed doors, and he had already run it twice round the newel post. As the four of them came up he drew the last link through, set a padlock in it, and snapped the padlock shut. Then he stood up and hung a card on the chain, square in the middle, where it would be read by anybody who came to the stair. The card was in his square capitals. *PROGRAM FLOOR CLOSED TO OBSERVERS. BY ORDER.*
chapter-17.md 127 Rooke answered with one word. Cael did not hear that either, but Ephram, who had come out of wing three behind the visitors and was close enough, told him afterward what it was. It was *No.* The Rhagen man had then asked, very politely, by whose order, since the card said *by order*, and Rooke had looked at him for a while and said, "Mine," and that had been the second word. Then Rooke turned his back on them and went in at the hall's side door, and a moment later the shutters began to close along the gallery's high windows, one after another, all down the north wall, from the inside.
chapter-17.md 235 He thought of three sheets he had seen on Bracken's counter that morning before the coach went down. Each had Withrow's name at its foot and the ink still wet: releases of the house's answer, entire, to three faculties of registry law that had written to ask for it to teach from. Across the bottom of the top one, under her signature, she had added a line in her own hand, *For teaching. Let them argue.* Under that, smaller, in Bracken's upright capitals, someone had written *REGISTRAR'S RESERVATION NOTED*, and then, smaller still and in the same hand, *and overruled.*
chapter-18.md 53 Karis went up to the reading room before the lamps went down and came back with the digest. She laid it on the residence table beside the receipt, open at its table of uses, took a pencil, and in the margin beside the line for *Statements entered (Silver and above)* wrote a small *45*, and drew a ring round it.
chapter-18.md 69 "I left the counter," he said. "Thirteen years and I have never left the counter. A first-year came to ask about a key and found nobody here." He dealt three to *OTHER*. "I have written it in the daybook. *Counter unattended, a quarter of an hour, by the registrar, on the registrar's own account.*" He dealt one to *FACULTIES*. "I was not going to leave that out."
chapter-19.md 177 Cael looked at the margin. The initials were a man's. Beside them, in a younger version of Withrow's hand: *two children; the house; Force, Bronze 4; haulage, river district; 31.*
chapter-20.md 5 They went in at the quarter to the eleventh bell, as they always did, on the key Rooke had lent Lira for the program's dawn drills and had told her he did not want to know about after dark. But the east hall was not what it had been. The shutters along the gallery had been closed since the Fourth-day by Rooke's order, and they did not need to be drawn. The chain still hung across the foot of the gallery stair with its padlock and its card, *CLOSED TO OBSERVERS*, so that Brom had to step over it to set the jug down on the bottom stair. The four lamps that the program now drilled under all day were standing at the corners of the painted crosses where the porters had left them, cold.
chapter-20.md 63 "No. It makes it mine." She went back to her cross. "Lay another. Not across this time. Lay it down the line, at me, and let me try to stand in it."
chapter-20.md 125 Cael did not write anything about it in words. On the back of his schedule, under the table, he wrote two numbers one above the other, *44* and *54*, the day the Archmarshal had been told the seat was full and the day the best practitioner alive had been laid on a pile. Karis saw them, and looked at him, and nodded once, and went back to her papers.
chapter-20.md 143 What Jent had already given away, Cael could read without looking back at his own notes, because the house's counsel had been keeping score of it in the margin of her privileged minute, where he could see it. Three headings stood there in her small capitals, written before the sitting began. *EXECUTION. PROVISION. RECORD.* Through each of them now ran a single neat line. She had drawn the first in Jent's first minute, when he told the hood the petition had never said the enrollment was wrongly made. The second, a little after, when he called the clause law and took the house's history of it for his own, word for word. The third when he said again, for the minute, that he had never read a better-kept record of an enrollment, and the clerk looked up to ask if that was to go in as spoken, and Jent said, "Every word. I'd decline to soften it if I were asked," and something along the front benches shifted.
chapter-20.md 169 Karis had slid a card along the table before the counsel stood. *NAMES. PREAMBLE. SHELF. TEXT.* "She's going from the people to the paper," she had breathed. "Tick them."
chapter-21.md 75 "No."
chapter-21.md 109 *SMALL ROOM, FIFTH-DAY, AFTER SUPPER, FIVE.*
chapter-21.md 175 *4 LOADED. 3 LIGHT. STAMPS STOP. 2 GO ON. 1 TURNS.*
chapter-21.md 213 Karis leaned over and tapped the chalk at the bottom of the column, under *1 TURNS*, without smudging it.
chapter-21.md 251 Lira got down off the table's corner, went to the shelf, and wrote under her own column in the porter's chalk, small: *40 — ABSENT, THEN FLED.* She stood back from it.
chapter-22.md 115 He had opened his eyes twice in the dairy, and the first time had been for the girl's chalk. *STAMPS STOP.* He knew that road. He knew, as a matter of his trade, what she did not: that a return like the one on the table had a route, office to office, and that the route ended where the stamps did. Past the last station there was no bag for it to go in, and no clerk to open it, and no drawer. A man could stand on the far side of that line and be watched by nobody whose watching went anywhere. He had never let himself think about it in those words before. He did not let himself go further now.
chapter-24.md 131 Cael opened the notebook on his knee and wrote the fourth line under the other three. *A false quarter. SW, by the rope. Eight feet, crossways. Under the sawdust at dawn.*
chapter-25.md 125 *For your file*, the steward's boy had said, handing it over after the finals, as if it were nothing; and nobody in her life had ever handed her anything *for your file* that she had wanted to keep. Her name and *Iron, Rank One, Wind*. His name, which she had decided she was allowed to know now, and *Silver, Rank Eight, Force*. Four lines of five filled. *Touch, confluence. Touch, Halcenvane. No touch. Touch, confluence. Two to one.* The panel's columns. *28.* *27.* And at the foot, pressed so hard into the paper that she could feel the letters from the back, Rooke's four words, which he had copied onto it at the inn without being asked.
chapter-26.md 185 The lodge-man had painted it himself in white on black, years before Bracken came to the hill, and he went over the letters every spring with a fine brush. *CARTS — ROUND BY THE LANE. CALLERS — SIGN AND WAIT. POST — THE BAG ON THE HOOK. ANYTHING SEALED — FETCH THE REGISTRAR.* Cael watched Withrow find the last line with her eyes and stop on it. The lodge-man had done exactly what his board said. Anything sealed had come up the road, and he had sent his boy for the registrar, and he had shut his window so that he could stand in his own door and see the whole of it.
chapter-27.md 125 "No."
chapter-27.md 135 Karis opened her notebook near the back and wrote *NO NAME*. Then she turned to the date a week ahead and entered *DISTRICT REPLY*, leaving the intervening leaves blank. Brom told her she was scheduling paper she had not seen. "I'm keeping room for it," she said.
chapter-28.md 35 He went back across the court to the wing in the half dark. The lodge-man's window was open again. The rain had got at the board beside it in the night, and somebody had already chalked *ANYTHING SEALED* in fresh.
chapter-28.md 133 "No. It's a correction. A mistake is when nobody wrote it down." Karis turned back to the copy. "Go on. You found it. That's the whole job."
chapter-28.md 191 "No." He had thought about that, too. "But it's a guess, and it's somebody's guess who knows. Somebody keeping her for the hard ones isn't keeping an Iron. And if it's Gold, I'm wrong, and the leaf says so, and I'll know I was wrong before I start." He took the pen. "Silver. Call it Silver. If anybody's got a better guess, I'll write that."
chapter-28.md 245 *NOBODY GOES IN AFTER HIM ALONE.*
chapter-29.md 7 *MOULD MENDED THIS YEAR. WIRE WENT AT THE TOP OF THE MARK. SEWED IT MYSELF. A SIXTEENTH LOW, I KNEW IT WOULD BE, THERE WAS NO HELPING IT. ONE RUN OFF IT SINCE FOR THE REGISTRY. WE CALL IT THE SECOND. PAID IN FULL.*
chapter-29.md 135 "No. That's how you'll know it's true."
chapter-29.md 241 "He asked for my room." She said it slowly, as if she were reading it off a page in a hand she did not know. "Out of every room on the hill." Then she looked up, and Cael saw that she was not frightened, or not only. "Yes. Tell him yes. Tell him I'll have the girl sent home early that day, and the kettle on, and every paper on the table turned over." She stopped. "No. Don't tell him the last part. I'll just do it."
chapter-30.md 121 Hesk's book was inside Cael's coat, against his ribs, where it had been every day since the gate. He could feel the corner of it when the coach rocked. He thought about the leaf at the front of it with four sets of initials, and *NOBODY GOES IN AFTER HIM ALONE* pressed so hard into the paper that you could read it from the back.
chapter-32.md 26 FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
chapter-33.md 25 *BROM, LIRA, KARIS, TWO HOURS EACH BY THE CLOCK ON THE SILL, AND THEN AGAIN FROM THE TOP.*
chapter-33.md 29 *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.*
chapter-33.md 85 "*Bill, not flag*," she said, and stopped, and looked at what she had written, and rubbed it out with her thumb. "No. I don't know. It's not answering wrong. It's not answering late. It's not answering at all, it's just moving." She looked at him honestly. "I don't know which. I'll write that."
chapter-33.md 101 Karis had gone, and Brom had gone to the infirmary to have his sling looked at, and for the quarter hour between their shifts Cael was alone in the room for the first time since the road, because the rota said *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.* and the healer had said that Brom's sling came first. He did not mind. He lay with his eyes shut and said *no* to the cup on the saucer and the lamp on the book, and counted the bell from the lecture range, and waited.
chapter-33.md 179 He stood in the doorway with his hands in his coat pockets and did not come in. He looked at Lira's foot on the stool and at Brom's sling and at the strapping round Cael's ribs where his shirt was open, and at the rota nailed to the inside of the door. He read the rota. Cael watched him read it, all of it, down to *SOMEBODY IN THE ROOM. ALWAYS.*, and then look at the doorway he was standing in, as if it had told him something he did not have words for.
chapter-34.md 5 The card was pinned at the board's head in Rooke's square capitals, as every card was. Under *LIRA* there were three lines, each with a date and a floor and a name. Rooke did not rub them out. He drew one stroke through each, level and unhurried, from the first letter to the last, the way a clerk strikes an entry he means to leave legible, and at the end of each line he wrote two words in the margin. Cael could not read them from the stair. He did not need to. He knew Rooke's two words for a bout not fought, because he had read them in that margin on other fighters' cards, and had never once expected to read them under hers.
chapter-37.md 138 FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
chapter-37.md 258 *Day 88. Five in Karis's room by three stairs across an hour. The case opened. What was in it does not go into a book. It is in a room with two keys, and the five of us have it, and that is where it will stay.*
chapter-38.md 81 "No."
chapter-38.md 337 She put the two flames out with a closing of her hand, and stood, and moved the line. She drew it again two feet further from him, and lit its ends again, and counted three and four, and the new thing asked, and he named it. She moved it two feet further and counted five and six, and it asked a little less surely, as a voice is less sure across a wider room. At ten feet he named it, and Karis wrote *10* in her notebook and drew a ring round it.
chapter-39.md 55 "The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
chapter-40.md 221 "No. I am not with anyone."
chapter-41.md 9 At the top the house was emptying: the outer court was full of carts, and the lodge-man stood at his window with the arrivals book turned to its other face, the departures, writing a name for every trunk that went under the arch. Third-years shouted to one another across the flags about the next term as if it were a country. Somebody had left a cracked practice lath leaning against the capped well with a paper on it that said *FREE*, and nobody had taken it.
chapter-41.md 225 "No."
chapter-43.md 103 "No. That's why you lost." He said it without any heat. "From tomorrow, every count ends on the right. Every one. I don't care where the line wants to go. You'll land on that foot until you've stopped asking it for leave, and then you'll land on it a hundred times more." He went back to the trestle and bent over the red book, and wrote one word under her name, and Cael, from the door, could not read it and knew what it was.
chapter-43.md 115 "No."
chapter-43.md 169 She turned the pages under the lantern. *FLOORS*, on the first leaf, in the capitals Karis hated. Sprung oak: six, and it gives you back more than you put in, so go early. Boards: five, and they're honest. Slate: four, and it takes the beat out of your knees by the third. New planks laid crossways: they slide, and nobody tells you. Chalk road in wind: four, and mind the ditch. She had underlined *ditch* twice and then, a week later, drawn a small rude face beside it.
chapter-43.md 171 *PEOPLE*, after that. A page for the Force: he builds on four, or on two when he's hurrying; you can see it in his knees. A page for the Silver at the confluence, with *go on your fifth* at the top in a box. A page for the Stone, which had almost nothing on it, because he never said anything, only a list of the places she had found him standing. Half a page for the Current girl. *Watch the front hip. Go along the bend.*
chapter-43.md 177 And then, on the leaf at the back that she had headed *ME* and had not much liked writing on:
chapter-44.md 57 At Cael's left the counsel's pen moved. She had her privileged minute open in front of her, and across the top of the page, in her small capitals, written before any of them sat down, stood three headings. *RENEWALS. NINE DAYS. EVALUATOR.* She drew a line through the first.
chapter-44.md 107 "No. I could have let you prove it, line by line, across two sittings. You would have. It would have been a pleasure to watch." He put his spectacles on to look at the sky, and took them off again. "But it would still have been right at the end of it, and I'd have known that from the start. I dislike sitting through an argument whose answer I already hold. It seems a discourtesy to the person making it."
chapter-44.md 129 "No." She let out a breath. "It isn't. Thank you." And she opened the wallet again, on her knees, and began to draft the covering leaf for Gault's notes while the coach climbed.
chapter-45.md 171 The panel's figures went up on the yard's board in the porter's boy's chalk. *Wind, 28. Blade, 26.* She did not look at them until the Current girl turned her round by the shoulders and made her.
chapter-48.md 125 "No." He did not smile. "It's a thing to ask you. I've had it ready since the tenth sitting's week, and I'm holding it until after the last Fifth-day. I'll not ask a man for anything while a seat has him. It wouldn't be a fair ask. You couldn't say no to me properly, and I'd never know whether you'd said yes."
chapter-50.md 249 "The first. *Return, from the chain of review, to the senior evaluation seat, on referral of the matter at Greyvane. Designation: Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*" The pause. "*Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*"
chapter-52.md 191 "I've got it written. I did it on the stair in the records hall, watching him square copies, and it's under *ME*." She read it as she would have read a bout sheet, quickly, with her finger under the line. "The program. That's a hall painted for me, and a red book from a river academy, and a Stone on loan who writes two lines to my coach every week. A year of it. The trial rule, and twenty-seven on a card, and then twenty-seven under a hall's seal at the confluence, against a Silver who told me afterward to go on my fifth. Twenty-eight on the oak against a Blade. Four losses on my sheet, and I know what every one bought." She turned the leaf. "A sitting. The first after the year's close, behind a blue door, with my name fourth on the board. Rooke chalked it over the week's drills and ruled a line under it so nobody could wipe it off. He's been building toward that door since the recess before this year, and he never once asked me to promise I'd walk through it."
chapter-52.md 199 "No. That's the bill. Here's the other side, because you asked for full price and that means both columns." She shut the book. "Nobody ever built anything to my height before. Fenmark had the form right and the wrong room. I walked out of there on my own feet and I've been proud of it ever since, and I thought that was all I'd ever want from a room: to be let leave it standing up. Then somebody built me a ladder, and I found out I wanted to climb. I said so, and you wrote it down. *Admit.*"
chapter-53.md 45 "No."
chapter-53.md 87 "No. I said in the term that I'd be annoyed if you thanked me, and I should be." She had her hand on the door. "The captain came to me at supper to ask for the Crown yard tomorrow at the fourth bell. I told him the yard was Rooke's to give and the bout was yours, and I see by your face that he has since asked you. I shall be in the stand. I have sat under a rug in other people's halls for two years to watch my own fighters, and I should like to see one thing on my own oak before the year closes."
chapter-53.md 177 *HE ASKED. DON'T BE KIND.*
chapter-53.md 195 *THE CROWN YARD. THE FOURTH BELL.*
chapter-53.md 197 *BLADE, IRON SIX. THE ENROLLEE.*
chapter-53.md 199 *ROOKE'S RULE: FOUR EXCHANGES, A TOUCH OR NONE IN EACH. TWO ENDS IT.*
chapter-53.md 201 *THE HILL AND THE TOWN.*
chapter-54.md 146 FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
chapter-55.md 29 *ON MY OWN FEET.*
chapter-56.md 47 The notes were real, and that had surprised her a little. She had meant to sit a quarter of an hour, for the form of it, and say her sentence. Then the grey woman had begun on what a table could see and what it couldn't, and Lira had found the pencil in her hand, because it was all true and nobody had ever said it to her so plainly. *A panel sees half of you.* She had turned to the back of the carter's book, under *ME*, and taken it down as fast as she could write.
chapter-56.md 149 "No."
chapter-56.md 159 "No. I'd have said so too." He stood up and put his arms through the pack's straps. "I've carried every letter they sent me for three years in one pocket. I find I can set one down."
chapter-56.md 191 "No. Thank you. They're mine to carry."
chapter-58.md 101 "No."
chapter-59.md 77 *By charter of the registry, this house being chartered: TIER VERIFICATION available at the district station, Ostrand, four days west. No station lies east of the river crossing, and the station at the ford is unstaffed. TRAVEL ENDORSEMENTS may be lodged at this house for forward transmission; allow twenty days. ENROLLMENT INQUIRIES by the same. The chartered rates of this house are posted within. Complaints of a chartered house lie to the district seat.*
chapter-59.md 95 It was in pencil, in the broad margin beside *Silver*, and it ran half the way down the sheet. *Nets, 14. At 2 the net. Less 3 owed from the grey mare.* A line was ruled under it, and a total, and the total had been crossed out and corrected. Whoever had done it had wanted a piece of paper with room on it, and had found one.
chapter-59.md 121 "No. I feel the hill in my legs."
chapter-60.md 153 "It was the only room on the sheet." Karis took a pencil from her notebook's spine and held it over the bare paper for a moment. Then she made a mark, small, and wrote beside it, in her exact hand, *ridge*. A finger's width on she made another. *Shelf; a pine; water at 36.*
chapter-60.md 167 Lira had her own book out by then, and had turned to the page with the capitals. *4 LOADED. 3 LIGHT. STAMPS STOP. 2 GO ON. 1 TURNS.* She had chalked that on a dairy wall once, and copied it out afterward, and carried it through eleven sittings.

