editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:97:The first two had gone to the others. A name to the house's counsel, which Cael heard and did not catch and which she answered with a short nod, as one tradesman nods to another across a street. A title, and the registry's measured bow, to Withrow. The third went past both of them to the girl behind them with the summary under her arm, and Karis's pen, which she had taken out to make a note of the room, stopped in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:185:"I thanked him first," she said, "so it would be on the minute before he gave everything away. Then it's on the minute that we heard the compliment and didn't bow to it." She folded the summary in half. "He agreed with all of it. In the first minute. I told you he would. And I still had to keep my face still. I'm telling you that so you know it isn't easy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:233:"In the examination hall. Three examiners at a table, and a slate in a wooden frame between them." She held her hands a foot apart. "They chalked it while I fought. Afterward the one in the middle copied it fair onto the form, and wiped the slate with his sleeve so the form would have no crossings-out. I remember thinking how careful that was." She put her hands down. "Every line on that form was correct. Wind. Copper. My name spelt right. Every step in the book, in the book's order. It was the room that was wrong. I've never been allowed to say both of those at once and have anybody listen. I only had *angry*, and angry's a very poor word. It's all one size."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:7:"Evaluation integrity," said Karis. "I've never once met that phrase in a hearing. Where does it live?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:125:"The house will answer," said Karis. "Because the other choice has its own word in the docket, and the word is *fled*, and this house is named on that paper, and I won't have *fled* written anywhere near it while I live here." She swept the six slips into one hand and tucked them into the back of her notebook. "I'll have bones on it in four days. And when the seat reads it, the seat will go through it with a pen and find nothing to mark. If the end's already fixed, I still want our tracks in the record, every step of them exactly where the code says a step goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:153:On the gallery stair Karis had begun to count, quietly, from the moment the dust ran. He tested the Wind at every number. It came late, and late, a beat behind his asking each time, until she said *eleven*, and then it came when he called it. Ten counts, the same bill as the first night. She wrote it down, and he laid another in the same place, and she began to count again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:167:Karis had started it as the third lane went down, as she had started the others, and he had been testing the Wind against her numbers. Somewhere about six he had stopped hearing them. Nothing had happened. The weight behind his eyes had simply stayed, warm and heavy, and the numbers had gone on past him like carts past a man asleep on a bench. He reached for the Wind out of habit, the way you test a stair, and it came late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:119:It was a small brass key worn bright at the bow, and it had lived in that pocket for longer than he had lived in any room. He held it on his palm in the pale square of light. Above him, on the shelf over the copying table, in the shadow, was the thing it opened. He did not look up at the shelf. He looked at the key.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:It was not in the hip and it was not in the shoulder. It was behind the eyes, a heaviness, as if somebody had laid a warm hand over the inside of his skull. And everything else he carried went slow with it. He reached for the Wind, out of curiosity, the way you test a stair, and it came, but it came late: a beat late, the way a dog comes when it has decided to come in its own time. He counted. For ten counts after the lane, everything answered him a beat behind his asking. At eleven it was gone, and the hand had lifted off the inside of his head, and the Wind came when he called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:203:"Nothing does." He had been turning it over himself since the count ran out. "Pull was the wrong thing answering. The push when I called the Wind, the read when I wanted my feet. That's gone. This is the right thing answering late." He thought back to the second floor in the dark, and the long while he had stood waiting for a pull that never came before he called anything at all. "At dawn I stood still after the lane until I was sure. I must have stood through the whole ten without counting them. Tonight was the first time I reached for anything inside them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:9:"Exhibition under the host's provision," the steward read. "Five exchanges at most. Two touches ends it. The panel rates for the figure only. For the confluence, Silver, Rank Eight, Force. For Halcenvane, Iron, Rank One, Wind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:There was a murmur at *Iron, Rank One*. Cael heard it go along the benches like wind going through barley. Most of the hall had read the board. Hearing it said aloud was different.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:163:Half a stride. Cael saw the distance exactly, because he had been watching her feet all afternoon and he knew to a hair where they should have been. Her lead foot came down a little more than a foot behind the place it was meant to land, and her palm, already out for his ribs, met air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:So Cael told her the fourth, and the sixth burst, and the half stride, a little more than a foot, between where her lead foot came down and where it should have come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:39:"An instrument of the registry, under seal," he read from the card, in a voice with nothing in it. "For joint service upon the Chancellor of Halcenvane Academy and upon the enrollee of record whom it names, the two together and in their own persons. Service to be acknowledged in both their hands. The seal to stay whole until it is." He lowered the card. "Are both parties present?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:41:"Both parties are present," said Withrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:7:Rooke sat at his trestle with the red book. The Stone sat beside him with his hands folded on his stomach, a broad patient man from the river academy who had come up the hill for the year to teach a Wind fighter to think in courses. Down at the near end, the program clerk had two ledgers open in front of her this morning instead of one. Cael noticed that, and noticed that she wrote in the second only when the gallery door opened, and he did not think anything of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:29:Lira finished the line. She bowed to the trestle, the short bow the program used, and looked up and found him at the rail, and lifted two fingers off her strapping: *two bursts, clean*. He lifted two back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:29:The grey coats had been on the landing that morning, both of them, the older pair the house had been watching since the start of the year. They had looked at her once as she came down the hill with tape round her wrists and a coil of cord over one shoulder, and then they had looked away again, at the road up to the gate, where the person they were paid to watch was not coming down. She was the program's Wind. She was nobody's subject.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:41:"Wind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:"Good springs," said the carter, nodding at the crate, and went to lead her horses down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:19:He let the read go all the way out and come all the way back, without stopping it anywhere short to look like timing. It brought him Lira's weight half a beat early, as it always did, and in lamplight half a beat was a long time. He used the Wind the way it wanted to be used and not the way the house's book had it. One burst, two, the second off the landing of the first; a third, low, along the wall; a fourth to take him round her lie on the four-count before her fourth arrived. On the boards in wing three he stopped at four, and never let a fifth show. Here he went to five without thinking about it, and then to six, and felt the old easy looseness come into his legs that he had not let himself feel on a floor in front of anybody since Norhold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:45:Cael reached for the Wind. It came late. It came a beat behind his asking, the right thing answering slow, as it had answered every time since the first night on this floor. He stood in it. *Bill, not flag.* Lira, beside him, breathing hard, was looking down at the boards where the dust was settling out of the band and the band was fading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:53:The Wind came when he called it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:153:Jent asked for a single word from the seat, *cognizable*: that this was a question the seat was allowed to pick up. Once it had picked it up, the code already said what came next. A frame. The standard protocol. And the enrollment would live or die by whatever the frame said back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:Seln touched the third bean. "The district seat. Twelve days ago. A query on the certification history of a Wind practitioner of this house, Iron Rank One, *for completeness of the respondent institution's file*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:*What I carry, at the year's open: seven confirmed, the seventh Daeva's storm, and the quiet thing from the stair among them, sealed. Under all seven, the still place I don't count. The storm still has its flag on it. Nine of ten, this last week, the Wind came when I called and nothing else came instead. Nine isn't ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:51:The Wind came first, Lira's burst. He set his feet at the old chalk mark by the north wall and felt the gather come low in the belly, the leading hip load like a drawn bow, and the shove at his back like a door slammed by a draught. The far boards came up to meet him twenty feet on. He landed, and the landing beat held him where he stood, the same short locked pause it had held him in for four years. He had spent four years trying to make it shorter. It was no shorter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:91:Gold was heavy. Everything else he carried had been shifting to make room for it, slowly, the way the furniture in a house shifts when somebody carries a great iron chest in through the front door. For weeks after Norhold he had reached for the Wind and got something else. Some mornings it was the push, and his whole body shoved at nothing. Once, on the road, it was the read, and he had stood in a waystation yard knowing the exact weight of the water butt and going nowhere. The healers had said a month. His own count had said more, and his own count had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:He turned round, slowly, and set his feet, and called the Wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:113:It came. The gather, the hip, the shove at his back, the boards coming up twenty feet on, the landing beat holding him. Only the Wind. Nothing came with it and nothing came instead of it. He stood in the landing beat with his breath short and his hip charging him for a sixth burst on a five-burst floor, and he was smiling in the dark like a fool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:119:The record was a public thing now, and it was large. Before Norhold he had carried six, and the registry had held five of them; the quiet thing was on no paper anywhere, and the storm did not yet exist. Only three of the five had been shown in public since he came to the bluff, at rates the house had chosen for him: six bursts on the oak, two contacts of the spark, and a read that looked like good timing. The push, where it showed at all, never went past a flat hand. Norhold had changed that for good. On finals day nine judges and three scribes had written down the Wind past six, and the push at a fighting load, and Reydan's give taking a Gold's fire, and the spark past its two contacts, and something none of their columns had a name for. Every one of those was on paper now in the tournament's own hand, and paper did not forget. But the house did not fight on finals day. On the bluff, in front of the wing's panels, he still fought at the rate the house had always shown, and a little under it, and never over it, and he scattered the figures inside that room so that they would breathe like an honest fighter's. He called it the band, and so did Lira. It was the oldest daily expense he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:135:*First-day, before light. The second floor. The full suite in order. Wind, five of five; the read, clean; the push, four, flat at the last; the give, the bag twice; the spark, one contact on the porters' basin; the lane, fifteen feet, a third deep, the dust ran. Then the Wind again, after the lane. It came alone.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:7:Bracken stood at the yard gate with the travel sheet on a board and read it aloud in his counter voice, a name at a time, with a tick for each. The Chancellor and the registrar, in the hired carriage. The coach. The program wagon. Rooke. The Stone. The program's two entrants, the Wind and the Current third-year from the movement floor, who was entered in the Iron draw. The house's research standing, with documents. Records support. Attendance, two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:Inside the oilcloth were two things. The registrar's letter, in a clear upright hand that wasted nothing, asking that the instrument constituting a certain office be supplied, together with the seal under which that instrument was itself issued. And a sheet of thin paper with a pencil rubbing on it of a seal, taken carefully, the grey of the lead built up in even strokes until the device stood out white: the registry's own pattern, the river and the scale, inside a beaded rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:95:Cael sat with his back to the horses and watched the house go up behind them into the black, the lodge window first, then the lecture range, then nothing. The wind had come round behind the rain and was drying everything it could reach. It was cold. The coachman had his collar up to his hat and was talking to the near horse in the low steady voice that never stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:171:The road itself was twenty-odd feet wide, cambered high in the middle so the water would run off it, with a ditch along the north side. It was dry, as the ferryman had said it would be. The wind came along the cut from the west behind them and lifted the chalk dust off the crown in little white skirls, and the horses' feet sent it up in clouds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:219:The fountain went on in the court below. The glass of the window was cold, and the cold came through it onto the leather of the satchel where it stood against the pane, so that when he put his hand on the flap it was cold too, like a hand that has come in from outside. The ribs were a low steady ache. The arm under its binding itched, which the healer had said meant it was mending. The new thing in him was quiet. Once, when the latch of the window settled in its keeper as the wind moved the frame, it asked; and he told it *latch*, and it was quiet again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:227:*Wind-adjacent — Lira, the burst. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere, the push. Iron-adjacent — Brom, the read, which found the fourteen. Compression-adjacent — Reydan, the give. Ember-adjacent — Karis, the spark, two contacts and no more. Shadow-adjacent — the stair, sealed, rent from waking. Storm-adjacent — Daeva, the lane; moved twice this week, a quarter hour and then forty minutes, and settled. Anchor-adjacent — the hand with no face; idle by naming; wants doorways.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:189:"You have something for this house," she said to the lead rider.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:91:He spent it late, as the line said: as her lead hand came in, two fingers laid on the back of her wrist for an instant and gone. The copper smell came up off his hand. Her wrist jumped as if a wasp had found it, and the strike went wide, past his ear, into the whitewash with a slap. She was not touched. There was nothing for the cards. But her glide had broken, and she had to step back off the wall to set it again, and while she was setting it the glass ran out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:He stood still and let her come. Glide. Step. Glide. On the third glide her front hip told him which way the strike would go, a quarter-beat early and no more. He went inside it. He did it with the second burst, but he spent it small, a short low burst of a stride and a half, so short that from above it would look like a quick step with good knees behind it and not like a Wind fighter's burst at all. That was a thing he had learned this year and had never written down anywhere except in the rotation's private margins: how to spend a burst so that it looked like less than it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:161:"There will be a line about observers in the minute of today's hours," he said. "I have written it. *Four officers of other houses present in the gallery, by the lodge book. The provision's demonstration conducted as on every other day.*" He looked at Cael over the case. "That second sentence is true. I wished it to be on the page in case anyone later wonders whether it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:There were twenty-three. She read every one, and she read the stamp at the foot of every one, and after the sixth she stopped reading the stamp aloud because everybody in the room could say it with her. A woman in the hill country whose Stone came up too heavy for its column. A ferryman's boy whose Current had something of Wind in it that his station had not written down. A girl at a guild hall in the south who did everything her Path said she should, the evaluator wrote, only as if she were older than her paper. Each report saw the same thing and asked for the same thing, and each came back stamped the same way. *Read against file. Enter at file.* Some carried *Continue observation* under the stamp in the evaluator's own hand, and some carried nothing, and one carried a single line that Karis read twice before she read it out: *Noted, and I do not like it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:3:Bracken wound the counter clock every Seventh-day before the first bell, and nobody had ever been allowed to talk to him while he did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:221:"At Norhold the wrong thing answered," he said. "In the middle of the floor. I called the Wind and something else came, and I went down. If I reach in the middle of a fight, that minute's going to happen again, or something like it. I won't be standing up for it. I may not know which way is down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:At Norhold the wrong thing had answered. Here nothing answered at all. He knelt in the dust and called his own legs, out of habit, to get him up, and the calling went out of him and found nobody at home. The Wind did not lift its head. The read lay where he had left it. Everything he carried had turned, all together and very politely, to look at the new thing standing in the doorway, and none of them was listening to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:Brom stood over him with his left arm hanging and his right hand open at his side, and did not say anything, and did not touch him. He stood the way he had stood in the channel. Anybody coming down the road would have had to come through him first. Nobody came. The wind went along the cut and the horses blew and stamped and the coachman talked to them. Somewhere down the bank the river roared through the narrows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:13:The read came first, as it always did. Then the Wind, a stir low in the belly. Then the rest. He called his legs and they came. He got one foot under him, and then the other, and Brom put out his right hand and Cael took it and came up off the chalk onto his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:114:He found the pencil and a clean leaf near the back and wrote it on the bank, with the chalk dust drying white on his hand and the wind turning the pages under his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:290:"Cracked," she said. "Two, I think. Not through. Eight days in strapping, and you'll know about every one of them." She began to wind the linen. "Chalk," she said, to herself, and that was the only question she asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:209:The third was a coastal station, a girl again. *Current, second rank at the frame. Keeper's note at the margin: reads into Wind, faint.* Enter: Current, Rank One.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:13:*An order presented, under a seal of the registry's pattern, naming an issuing office unknown to this counter. Read aloud in the court at the Chancellor's request, in full, by the lead rider.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:35:"*An offer was made by the lead rider to the enrollee,*" said Bracken. "*In these words.*" He gave them exactly. Cael could have given them himself. "*The order is addressed to two parties. The second may come with us of his own accord. If he comes voluntarily, nobody here need refuse anybody anything, and we can all be gone before the third bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:169:"So does your Wind," said Lira, "and nobody blames you when it knocks a tray off a table on the oak." She settled her foot on the stool. "Write that it was in you and it wasn't you. Both. They're both true, and I was the one in the doorway, so I get to say which goes first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:241:Somebody had shut the window in the night against the wind, and its latch had dropped into the keeper with a small iron click that he had heard in his sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:265:He priced it, lying there, because that was what he did with anything new. A word a thing. A word cost almost nothing; a word was cheaper than the Wind's landing beat or the read's quiet minute after. But this house alone had more doors in it than he could count from his bed, and there were other houses, and roads with gates on them, and inns, and every door was going to need its word. It would never be paid off. It would be a small coin he put down at every threshold he ever crossed, so many times a day that one morning he would look up and find he had stopped noticing his hand go to his pocket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:288:"I said nine weeks and I said I didn't believe it." She turned back through the notebook to the page on the Second-day, the leaf they had all initialled, where *about nine weeks, Karis's curve, not believed* stood in his own hand. She laid the new sheet beside it. Then she turned back further, to the column she had kept for the storm since Norhold, and further, to the old figures she had copied out of the old volume, the little points low on the paper from the Wind and the read and the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The house's farm lay on the back of the bluff, on the long south slope away from the river, where the wind was less. Below the stable passage were the kitchen gardens in their walls, and below the gardens the upper orchard and the lower, old trees in rough grass with their branches bare and black with wet. Below the lower orchard a stream came down out of a fold in the slope and ran along the bottom hedge toward the water meadows, loud over stones. The farm track went down beside it, and at the orchard's foot, where the track met the stream, stood a small stone cottage with smoke going up from it, which was the gardener's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:93:"Of the shortest person present," said Seln, reading it upside down. "That is the rule as I was taught it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:105:"When you come to a place where people live," said Seln, at the top of the track, "you come the way the track comes. In the middle of it, at a walk, in daylight if there is any left. Your hands are where a man at a window can see them. You stop a little way off, where he can look at you for as long as he likes, and you let him look, and you do not speak until he has." He nodded down the track at the cottage. "He will decide what you are in the time it takes you to walk forty yards. You cannot stop him deciding. You can only give him an easy thing to decide."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:271:*Sixth-day, first bell. Program floor. Wind, Iron One, v. Current, Iron Four. Rated. Program trial rule.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:101:"Present," said Withrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:109:"Present," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:189:The long room stood when the seat rose. Cael was on his feet behind Withrow's chair, and so he saw her bow from behind: the line of her grey collar going down, and stopping, and the back of her neck held straight. He had watched her bow on the house's steps to visiting officers for two years and could have told its depth with his eyes shut. This was the same. It was the registry's own measure, the one she had been taught at twenty and had given across a platform like that one for twenty years from the officers' side, and she gave it now to a hood with no face under it as she would have given it to a registrar she had dined with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:7:The lead did not move. He stayed by the cart's wheel with one hand lifted, keeping the time, and sent the other four in. They came from the two ditches and the two ends, one at each corner of the coach, unhurried, with their staves still at their belts and their hands open. *One.* Their feet touched the chalk at the same instant. *Two.* The lead's voice went along the cut like a man calling a dance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:17:The lead's voice did not falter. He said *four*. But there were only two men on their marks for it, and a ring with two men in it is a pair of men.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:29:He spent the Wind at ten paces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:31:It was a good burst. He had been holding the band down so long that the full thing came out of him almost with relief: the gather low in the belly, the hip loading like a drawn bow, and the shove at his back. The road came up under him. Six feet out from the place he had left, it stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:255:He knelt in the road. The wind came along the cut from the west and blew the chalk dust off the crown in a long white skirl and carried it away east over the culvert.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:9:The queue was four long. A first-year girl stood at the front of it with a folded paper in both hands, holding it the way you hold a thing you have been told twice not to lose. She was small, with a scarf wound three times round her neck against the cold and a Wind badge on her coat so new its pin still shone. The desk clerk was busy with the day book, so it was the teaching assistant who rose from the copying table to take it from her, as he did when the clerk was busy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:27:It was a decision; he felt himself make it, the way he felt himself set his feet before a burst. He did not stop knowing. He only put the knowing where he put the band, somewhere it could be carried every day without being looked at. The girl with the new badge was a Wind first-year with her scarf trailing. That was what she was this morning. If it was not all she was, that was not a thing he could mend from a queue, and it was not a thing he was going to do to her by looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:279:He knew what would be done with it. The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld; he had cited that section himself, once, long ago, when a guild hall's assessor would not sign. His line would stop nothing. The sittings would sit, and the ruling would be read, and somewhere in a minute a clerk would enter that a thing usually present was not there. That was the whole of what he could do, and he had weighed it on a post road for four days and found that it was still worth doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:93:On the steps, in a thin bright wind, the counsel stopped with her bag in her hand and looked up at Karis, who was a head taller and looked, just then, about twelve years old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:That had been the last morning, in a room over a ship's chandler's on a quay, with six young men on benches who were not yet anything, and a cat asleep in the sun on the window-ledge. She had been old even then. She had opened every lesson by telling them that nobody in that room would ever be told the whole of anything and that the sooner they stopped minding the better; she had spent the weeks between on the craft, how to read a subject, how to write a return that was true and useful and no longer than it needed to be, how to live a year in a town and leave nothing behind but a room that had been let. The grades she had kept for the end, so that they would know what the other side of the table did, and where they themselves stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:147:He nodded. He bowed, not to any of them, but to the room, a short stiff bow from the waist of the kind evaluators gave a sitting before they took a chair. Then he took the chair across the table from Karis, the one with its back to the window, and sat down with his hands on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:183:The clock on the landing ticked. Through the floor came the sound of the weight on the boards again below, and somebody laughing, and a door. The singer on the floor above had stopped, and Cael did not know when. Out of the window, across the slate roofs, the green cupola of the wool exchange stood in the grey afternoon, four streets off, with a thin smoke going up behind it from somebody's chimney in a straight line, because there was no wind. Brom breathed, slow and even, the way he breathed in a long hold at the frames. The cold stove ticked once, the way iron does when the day changes round it. Cael's ribs, which the healer had called mended a week ago, reminded him where they had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:227:For a moment he stood behind the chair with both hands on its rail, and his eyes went once round the borrowed room: the oblongs in the dust, the pale squares on the plaster, the stove gone cold, the cupola in the tall window with its straight thread of smoke. Then he made his bow again, the short stiff one from the waist, and made it to the room, and to nobody in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:89:They fought it on the Crown yard's sprung oak in the afternoon, under a white sky, with the house on the tiers. Rooke had chalked it on the yard's board himself. *Exhibition. Wind, Iron One, Halcenvane. Blade, Iron Five, the lake house. Five exchanges at most. Two touches ends it.* Three cards at the table: the lake house's instructor, the Mire instructor from the wing, and the Stone, who sat between them with his big hands folded and looked as if he had been carved there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:171:The panel's figures went up on the yard's board in the porter's boy's chalk. *Wind, 28. Blade, 26.* She did not look at them until the Current girl turned her round by the shoulders and made her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:179:*Advancement evaluation, Silver bracket. The district seat, Ostrand. The first sitting after the year's close. Program's entrant: the Wind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:269:She came straight at him, burst on burst, her second and her third with no count between them, and he called the Wind to go and it came a beat behind his asking, and he called the read and it came late, and he got his forearm up and opened the give and it opened *late*, and by then her palm was against his ribs, on the right side, exactly where they had been cracked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:Cael heard it, because he was watching her. She had come off the end of a long flat curve and put her palm on the man exactly where she had meant to, the best touch she had made all season, and the steward said "Touch. Halcenvane" in his ordinary voice, and the north benches, which ten minutes ago had been on their feet for the house's Wind, did not make a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:9:Cael followed their faces. The front row had turned, all along its length, toward the west end of the hall, the way a field of barley turns all one way in a wind. Behind them the second row had stood to see over the first. Across the square on the south side people were craning, and in the little loft over the west doors Seln had his head turned, and had had it turned, Cael realised, for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:87:The Silver was walking it too, from the other end, with his hands behind his back and his guild sash over his shoulder instead of knotted at his hip. He was not fighting again; the exhibitions were done. He stopped when he saw her, and she stopped, and they stood in the corridor between the stacked trestles, a bargeman and a Wind, with the river going past behind the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:125:*For your file*, the steward's boy had said, handing it over after the finals, as if it were nothing; and nobody in her life had ever handed her anything *for your file* that she had wanted to keep. Her name and *Iron, Rank One, Wind*. His name, which she had decided she was allowed to know now, and *Silver, Rank Eight, Force*. Four lines of five filled. *Touch, confluence. Touch, Halcenvane. No touch. Touch, confluence. Two to one.* The panel's columns. *28.* *27.* And at the foot, pressed so hard into the paper that she could feel the letters from the back, Rooke's four words, which he had copied onto it at the inn without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:The east hall had its two lamps lit and its shutters down. Outside it was not yet light, and a wet wind was leaning on the north wall, so that the long room seemed smaller than it was, a lit box with six people in it and the dark pressing on the glass. The program's clerk had moved her trestle out from the wall to the edge of the painted floor and sat at it with the red book open and two pens laid ready. Rooke sat on her right with his board. On her left the Stone stood with a flag in each hand, a red and a blue, held down along his thighs like a man carrying two fish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:15:"Program trial," said Rooke. "My rule. You both know it, and I'll say it anyway, because it goes in the book." He did not get up. "Four exchanges. A flag to whoever takes each one, or to nobody. Most flags has it. Level after four, the table rates you both. Three bursts for the Wind; past three the card pays. The Current, what her Path allows." He nodded at the clerk. "Rated. In this book. Go when the Stone drops his hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:45:The flags dropped, and she stood for one beat with her weight on both feet, and on the second she simply went. There was no lie in it at all. Burst two, flat and hard, straight across the Current girl's glide at the hip, the plain rude line that every drill in the red book told a Wind never to take against a Current, because you would arrive before she had bent and she would bend round you.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:117:She wound the tape round her fingers into a ball. He waited. He had thought, on the First-day, that it would keep, and that it was Rooke's to say, and that she had earned one morning of being pleased. All of that had been true. It did not seem like very much, standing here.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:169:She turned the pages under the lantern. *FLOORS*, on the first leaf, in the capitals Karis hated. Sprung oak: six, and it gives you back more than you put in, so go early. Boards: five, and they're honest. Slate: four, and it takes the beat out of your knees by the third. New planks laid crossways: they slide, and nobody tells you. Chalk road in wind: four, and mind the ditch. She had underlined *ditch* twice and then, a week later, drawn a small rude face beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:201:Wind, first rank. That was what the paper said. What she was, she was writing down herself, in pencil, in a carter's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:He did not put it in the book. It was only a column in his head, and each day it was shorter than the day before. On the first day it had run to a dozen things: metalled stone underfoot, ditches, dressed milestones with the district's device, a keeper at the ford with a stamp, a chartered sign, a board of hours. On the second day there had been a post bag on a hook, sealed in lead, and a notice of tolls in the registry's print, and a rider in district grey going west at a trot, who had not looked at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:51:They came to the top in the last of the light. The land lay over on its back there, wide and bare and rolling, with the wind going across it, and half an hour on stood a low building with a lamp in its window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:The board hung from an iron arm over the door, with the registry's seal burned into it, and the wind had swung it against the wall so often that it had worn a pale groove in the stone. There was a yard with a wall round it, and a well with a lid, and a stack of cut turf against the gable, higher than a man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:61:He was old, and bent a little to one side, as a thorn bends that has had the wind on it all its life. He held the lantern up and looked at the horse first, and then at the cart. He walked round to the tailboard and lifted the canvas's edge with two fingers, as a man looks under a cloth at a market, and let it drop again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:125:The road went on across the upland. There was no stone of any kind now, only the track, two ruts and a ridge of grass, and the wind, and now and then a sheep that lifted its head. A little before noon they came to a pillar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:The wind went over the roof, and the dark block sat in its notch with its mark on it, as it had sat before anybody built a wall there to keep the rain off a clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:325:It was very quiet, for the wind had dropped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:401:"That's a post-rider," said Seln. "Not the registry's. There is no lead on the bags."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:459:They stood on the ridge in the wind, the five of them, and looked at an old brown horse. He had been bought for a hill a day, and he had done one each day, honestly, for five days, and this was a second in one morning, after a river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:479:He did not see it coming. The track went up between two rocks, and then there was no more up, and the wind hit him in the face. He walked forward three paces to get out of the cart's way, and looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:485:There was a valley below, deep and long, with a thread of water in it. Beyond the valley was another ridge, higher than this, dark with trees he could not name from here, and beyond that another, paler, and another after that, the colour of smoke; he counted seven before the air took them. There was no road on any of it but the two ruts at his feet. He saw no smoke, and no roof. The wind came over it from somewhere that nobody had drawn, and it smelled of nothing he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:245:"Of yours," said Seln. "You are the shortest person present. I shortened my stride, and it took me three attempts to get it right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:283:The second day was longer than the first, and wetter underfoot, and the stones went by. *Twenty-four. Twenty-five.* The road was still metalled, and still ditched. At a place where another road came in from the south, there was an iron post with a hook on it and a leather bag hung on the hook, sealed with the registry's lead. A rider would come for it, and leave an empty one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:33:"I saw. I mean after. On the ferry." She pulled her coat round her. A thin cold rain had begun to come in sideways off the river, hardly more than a wetness on the wind. "Did anybody look at you differently?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:57:In his room, Hesk's clock on the shelf said ten minutes short of the eighth bell, and the lecture range had rung the eighth while he was on the stair. It had lost its usual minutes since the morning, in its usual direction. He set it right and wound it, and lay down, and was asleep before he had finished counting the things he did not have to do tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:63:He learned it on the First-day morning, crossing the outer court to the refectory, when the fountain went up and came down and he could hear it do both. On an ordinary morning you could not hear the fountain at all; there were too many boots on the flags and too many voices under the covered walk, and somebody always shouting for somebody from a window. This morning there was the fountain, and the wind coming over the north wall off the river, and a pigeon on the lodge roof saying the same thing over and over, and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:103:Cael read it on his sill in the grey afternoon, with the window open a crack because his room was always too warm in the afternoons, and the wind coming in off the river and moving the corner of the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:"The same quiet. Read again." He closed the book and gave it back to the lodge-man through his window with a word of thanks. Then he walked with Cael out under the arch and along the outer wall, where the wind came up the bluff off the river, and spoke to the wall rather than to him. "They have not gone. They have stood down. There is a difference. A man who has gone has decided he was wrong. A man who has stood down has decided he does not need to do anything, because what he wants is coming to him by itself." He walked a little way. "The corridor is on schedule. Every sitting sits. Every form is filed. Nobody on this hill has put a foot wrong, and so nobody has to come up the road and make one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:3:Bracken wound the counter clock on the Seventh-day before the first bell, and nobody had ever been allowed to speak to him while he did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:17:She read the first leaf from its head to its foot, which took her no time at all. *Withdrawal of an enrollee from a chartered house.* Her name stood on it in Bracken's small upright hand, and under it *Wind*, and *Iron, Rank One*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:87:He had not blotted it. He turned the leaf on the counter, so that its left margin lay toward him, and he looked up at the clock he had just wound, and then he dipped the pen himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:149:"Lapsed." She put her hand to her collar and drew out the cord. The key was on it, brass, worn bright at the bow by a year against her skin, and she lifted the cord over her head, and held it, and for a moment she did not put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:"Tomorrow, at the first bell, I shall open the daybook and enter the post. On the next Seventh-day I shall wind that clock. In the week after the close a clerk will come up the road with a stamp for the chancellor's folder, and I shall see that he is handed the right volume and initials his own line." He took the last pair of glasses off his forehead and laid all three in a row. "I tell you so because you might suppose, after this year, that a man in my chair would go about it differently. I have asked myself whether I ought. I intend to keep true records, as I have kept them, and four files will go down to the district in tomorrow's bag with not a line in them that I could not swear to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:183:"There is one thing more," said Bracken, in his ordinary voice, and put one pair of glasses back on. "The evaluator of record was at this counter before I had finished with the clock. He does not speak to me while I wind it; he is the only officer of the house who has never needed telling. He left a leaf from his book, which is his last note upon the provision's enrollee, and he asks that it be entered with the withdrawal. He asked also that you should read it here, before I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:103:*Present duty.* He wrote it as his order had it: *Records, junior chair, institutional proceedings at Ostrand; the seat's Fifth-days, until relieved.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:"*The seat, having heard the petition and the responses in full, and having found the enrollment lawful in its execution and the respondent institution compliant with its charter throughout — which findings stand undisturbed — nonetheless finds the evaluation-integrity question sustained on the merits. The demonstration provision describes the practitioner; it does not evaluate him within the meaning of the integrity provisions. The petition for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol is granted. The re-evaluation shall be conducted at the district Arbiter station within five weeks. Should the re-evaluation return no valid classification, the procedure of the code's chapter fourteen shall apply: nullification, suspension, asset-restriction. The demonstration-provision enrollment is revoked effective at the close of the present academic year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:155:He folded the finding along its folds and laid it on the table. He did not sign it, and he did not tear it, and he did not say that he refused it. He put it down as a man sets down a thing he has decided not to carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:203:"I've wanted it all year. I thought the thing I wanted lived on that floor, and I've looked for it there every dawn since the first week." She put the book away inside her coat. "Turns out it doesn't live here. It came up the road with me. I landed on a capstan bar in a wool yard two days ago on whichever foot I liked, with nobody holding a card, and it was all there. It's in my feet. They can't keep it in a hall, and they can't revoke it." She looked at the last slip. "But the ladder lives here, and I'm leaving the ladder. I want that written at full price, and I don't want anybody telling me it's cheap because I can still jump."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:205:He wrote it. *The program, the sitting, Rooke. Full price. It doesn't live here.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:5:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:101:"I went and looked at the district hall yesterday," she said. "From outside. It's three streets over. It's got a blue door, and a board beside it with the sittings on, and the first one after the year's close has six names. Mine's the fourth. Somebody's written *Wind* after it, and then *Halcenvane*." She picked at the tape on her wrist. "I stood there a good while. A carter asked me if I was lost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:241:"The scope of this seat was minuted at the third sitting, in the presiding seat's words," said Ilsev. She spoke slowly, with a small pause at the end of every clause, and three pens went with her. "It has kept within them. Within that scope the evaluation seat is obliged to lay before the proceedings any matter upon the subject's file that would govern the conduct of an evaluation. There is one such matter. The file carries a designation. The petitioner has set it out in the second schedule to his petition, at the fourteenth line. A designation of that kind governs who must be present at an evaluation, and to what office its result is sent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:141:She had never quite let herself say it in so many words. She had said *the program*, and *Rooke's floor*, and once, to Cael on a stair, *heavy*. But the plain fact was that a man had taken a hall, and had it planed, and sent to a river academy for a red book, and had a painter put four hundred marks on the boards, each one where a Wind fighter of exactly her reach would want it. Nobody had built anything to her height before. Fenmark had built a room for a girl it had imagined, and when she had turned out not to be that girl it had put her outside the room with her form correctly filled in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:305:The Wind first, because it was the oldest: low and flat off the left foot, straight down the middle of the floor. The oak took the landing and threw him on, as it was built to, and he was in the air again before the beat had finished. Three. Ephram's hands had come off the grip, and he had the lath up, and his heel was going down, and the read had all of it, deep, to the hips, a full beat before it happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:339:*Two. Three.* Three cuts in a chain, heel and hip and edge. Cael asked for the Wind and it came after he had asked, and he got out from under the first by the width of a sleeve. *Four.* The second he took on his raised left forearm, with the give open, and it opened late, and the edge went through it. *Five.* He had been right about that too. The give was a cup, and a cup holds a blow, and this was not a blow; it was a line of cold drawn across the bruise that was already there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:343:*Six. Seven.* The third was the short one, with no hip in it. He saw the heel go down, and he knew, and he could do nothing with the knowing for a whole slow beat. He spent a burst he could not afford. Seven. It was one past what the oak gave free, and it took him out sideways along the rim wall with the lath's wind on his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:9:That was not, in itself, a thing to write down, for a man could be late. But the clerk sat with his pencil lifted, and could not go on to his column. In two years the teaching assistant had never once been late. He had never been early either. He had come in by the inner door at the same moment each day, without a sound, so that the clerk had mostly not noticed him arrive, and had only looked up and found the table occupied, as one finds that a clock has been wound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:185:Forty paces. It was his own rule, and he had given it in an orchard with the reasons: water covers a voice at forty paces and a foot at ten, and things walk to water at night. He had told them it was to be forty paces of the shortest person present, and the researcher had written in her notebook, quite seriously, *of whose?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:"Because every one of them costs," he said. "The Wind's bill comes to the hip. The push comes out of the right shoulder, four blows a sitting and no more. The read is cheap, and the give is a cup that fills. Karis's spark burns me too, at the fingers, if I spend it past two contacts. The storm's the heaviest thing I own, and when I lay a lane the whole of me goes a beat slow afterward for ten counts, and if I lay three close together the third costs twelve." He said it in the log's voice, each figure exact. "Those are the prices, and they're all in the book. Nothing's free. That was the first thing I learned, on a cot at Ardenmere with the Log on my knee, and it's still true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:160:"The night before the final," said Cael. "In a back room on the harbour, with the shutter open a crack. We'd costed everything I was going to spend the next day: the Wind past six, the push at fighting load, the give, the spark. Everything that had been held back for two years was going to go out in one afternoon, in front of the whole continent, and be on the record for good." He looked at Lira. "And I said the quiet thing stayed sealed, and Lira said to cost the seal, out loud, and say why."

