     1	# Chapter 12 — Admit
     2	
     3	The east hall had never held a crowd before, and on the thirtieth morning of the year it held one.
     4	
     5	Nobody had announced the trial beyond a line on Rooke's own board, chalked the evening before in his square capitals: *PROGRAM TRIAL. SECOND-DAY, SECOND BELL. LIRA / FORCE THIRD-YEAR. RATED.* But the house had read it, and the house knew what *rated* meant on that floor. It meant the first bout ever fought on the program's painted boards under the program's own seal, with a figure at the end of it that would go into the program's own book. By the half-bell the gallery was full along both sides. By the second bell the porters had opened the shutters on the high windows to let the heat out, and the light came down onto the white crosses in long bright bars, and there were people standing on the gallery stair.
     6	
     7	He had found Lira an hour before it, sitting on the east hall's outer step in the grey light with her back against the doorpost and a roll of tape in her lap, doing her wrists. She did them slowly, as she never did on an ordinary morning, a turn at a time, pressing each turn flat with her thumb before the next went over it.
     8	
     9	"Brom's way," she said, when she saw him look. "It takes longer. It gives you something to do with your hands that isn't shaking." She tore the tape with her teeth. "They're not shaking. I'm just telling you why it works."
    10	
    11	He sat down on the step below her.
    12	
    13	"You asked me once not to count your bursts," he said.
    14	
    15	"I did. Ages ago." She started the other wrist. "I still don't want you to. I'll know how many. I'll be the one paying for them." She pressed a turn down. "Count his."
    16	
    17	"His what?"
    18	
    19	"Anything. His stamps. His settles. How long he laughs. You'll see things from the rail I won't see from the floor, and I won't be able to ask you about them till it's over, and I want them all, in order, when I do." She finished the wrist and flexed the hand, and looked at it as if it belonged to somebody she was fond of. "Karis will count my bursts whether I like it or not. That's her business. Yours is him."
    20	
    21	Down the walk the Force came round the corner of the range with two of his cohort, all three of them laughing at something, and saw her on the step, and lifted a hand. She lifted one back. Then she stood, and went in to the floor without another word, and Cael went up the gallery stair to find the rail.
    22	
    23	Cael had the rail at the north end, where he had stood on the second morning of the year to watch the red book. Brom stood on his left with his arms folded. Karis stood on his right with her notebook shut and her pen in her hand. Ephram was three steps down the gallery stair with the captain's file under his arm, where he could see the whole floor and be seen by the whole cohort, which Cael supposed was the point. The girl from the reading room's outer table had found a place against the far wall of the gallery, with her small notebook open.
    24	
    25	Below them on the floor stood the rating table: a trestle under the north windows with three chairs behind it. The clerk with the red drill-book sat at one end. Rooke sat in the middle with his board and his pencil. And at the other end, with a ruled card in front of him and his hands folded on the table, sat the Stone from the river academy, who had asked to come and teach the girl who beat him and was now going to put a figure on her.
    26	
    27	Rooke stood up at the second bell and said the rule, once, in a voice that carried to the back of the gallery without being raised.
    28	
    29	"Program trial. My rule, not the region's." He held up his board as if the rule were written on it, which it was not. "Four exchanges. Each one, the table gives a flag to whoever took it, or to nobody if nobody did. Most flags takes the trial. Three bursts a bout for the program's entrant, as on every day she's ever stood on this floor. Past three, the table still gives the exchange to whoever takes it, and the card pays for every burst over. The other fighter, what his own Path allows. Level after four, the table rates them both, and the higher figure takes it." He sat down. "The figure goes in this program's book. Not the registry's. Not yet."
    30	
    31	Lira stood at the south chalk with her wrists taped and her left arm carried a little close, out of habit. Across forty feet of painted boards stood the Force.
    32	
    33	---
    34	
    35	He was a big young man, broad in the back and thick through the thighs, and he stood at his chalk with his feet planted and his hands loose and a pleasant expression, the expression of somebody who has been looking forward to a thing and has decided to enjoy it. He had been drilling beside Lira every morning since the recess. He knew the four-count; he had run it himself, badly, for the practice of seeing it. He had laughed once on this floor when she lied to him on the second count and he went the wrong way. He was Iron Rank Seven, six ranks above her on the porter's wall, and he was the best thing the cohort had built in a straight line since Ephram.
    36	
    37	Cael watched his knees.
    38	
    39	From the rail there was nothing else to watch. A Force fighter's shove went on contact, and this one's contact was the floor. He stamped with his front foot, and a beat later the boards ahead of the stamp, out to a stride, jumped, as a table jumps when somebody slaps it from beneath, and went on jumping for a count or two before they lay down. Then the shove came back up into him along the line it had gone out by, and his knees gave and his hips dropped a finger's breadth while he took it. That was the settle. Cael could see it from the rail and hear the boards answer it. It was the bill for the last stamp, paid before the next.
    40	
    41	"Exchange," said the clerk.
