    78	
    79	The third count, nothing happened.
    80	
    81	It was the hardest thing in the world to watch. Lira stood on the painted floor with one foot forward and one back and did nothing at all, for a whole count, while a Rank Seven Force stood twenty feet away with the boards still jumping in front of him and his knees bent, waiting. The gallery made a small sound. Somebody laughed, uncertainly. The Force laughed too, from surprise, the way he always did, a short bark.
    82	
    83	Then his knees gave, and his hips dropped.
    84	
    85	Cael saw it. Lira saw it a hair before he did. The settle was on the man's own fourth count, his own rhythm, the same rhythm he had paid to in the first exchange with nothing in his way. He had to take his own shove back, whether she had given him anything to shove at or not.
    86	
    87	And her fourth count was the same beat as his.
    88	
    89	She burst off the back foot. Not the front foot she had lied with, which stood on boards that had jumped a moment ago; the back one, which had never left wood he had touched. She burst at the angle the first three counts had sworn she would never take, low and to the right, past the place he had stamped, and came down on still boards two strides from his left hip with her landing beat holding her steady. The Force was still in his settle, knees bent, taking his own push back, with nothing to put it into.
    90	
    91	She touched his shoulder. Lightly. Two fingers.
    92	
    93	The Stone lifted his flag. Blue.
    94	
    95	The noise in the gallery was not cheering, not at first. It was the sound of a great many people realizing at the same moment that the story they had been watching was a different story. Then it was cheering. Rooke, at the table's middle, put his pencil to his board and wrote one word, and Cael could not see what it was and did not need to.
    96	
    97	"Two bursts," said Karis, writing. "Two exchanges. One flag each."
    98	
    99	The Force stood a moment looking at the place on the boards where he had stamped for a girl who was not there. Then he shook his head, grinning, and went back to his chalk.
   100	
