   197	"The sitting's tomorrow," Bracken agreed. "The seat does not adjourn for weather. I have never known it to, and the house has never in my time asked it to." He looked out at the water for a moment longer. "Come and see the coachman. He's in the stable passage with a map and an opinion."
   198	
   199	The house's coachman had driven the delegation coach for as long as Cael had been on the hill. He was a short broad man with grey whiskers who talked to his horses in a low steady voice all day and to people hardly at all. He had a road map of the valley spread on an upturned bucket in the stable passage, weighted at the corners with four horseshoes, and he put one thick finger on it when they came in, as if he had been waiting with it there.
   200	
   201	"Up this side," he said. "West, along the bank road. Two hours if the bank road holds, and it will, it's high. To the upper ferry at the narrows." The finger moved. "She runs in any water. Rock both sides, no width for the river to throw her about in. Over there. Then back east down the river road on the far side, two hours more, into the city by the west gate." He looked up at Bracken. "Leave at the hour before light. In by noon. Sitting's after."
   202	
   203	"Thank you," said Bracken.
   204	
   205	The coachman folded the map along its old folds and put the horseshoes in his pocket and went back to his horses.
   206	
   207	That evening, in the refectory, Withrow came to the end of their table on her way out and stood behind Karis's chair with one hand on its back.
   208	
   209	"Since the gate, no enrollee of this house leaves the hill alone," she said. "You know that. Tomorrow you are five, and the house's counsel went down to her rooms by the wharf this afternoon while the bridge was still open, and will meet you at the side door." She looked along the table at each of them, the way she looked at a page before she signed it. "I would go with you, if the seat had asked for me. It has not. It has asked for him, and the maker of the copies, and the advocate of record. Go the long way and come back the short one."
   210	
   211	She went out.
   212	
   213	"The long way," said Lira, to her plate.
   214	
   215	"It's only a road," said Brom.
   216	
   217	He came up to Cael's room an hour later with his kit bag, all the same, and turned it out on the bed.
   218	
   219	It was what he took to every floor that was not the house's own. Two rolls of linen strapping, a roll of the stiff kind that the healers used for wrists, a tin of the porter's horse salve that smelled of tar and worked better than anything the infirmary had, a clean neckcloth, a flask, a heel of bread wrapped in a napkin, and a short length of leather with a buckle at each end that Cael had never seen him use and had never asked about. He went through all of it with his big careful fingers, as he did before a meet, and put it back in a different order.
   220	
   221	"It's a sitting," said Lira from the window seat. "They'll want him to say *They are mine* and sign. Nobody straps for that."
   222	
   223	"I strap for the road," said Brom.
   224	
   225	She did not laugh at him. That was how Cael knew she had been thinking about the road too.
   226	
   227	Karis came last, with the attestation wallet under her arm and her coat already over the back of the chair as if she meant to sleep in it. She checked the wallet's contents leaf against its contents, which she had done at supper and would do again in the coach, and then she sat on the end of the bed beside Brom's kit and looked at the sill.
   228	
   229	There was nothing on the sill but Hesk's book and the lamp. The case had gone back down the stair under Seln's coat an hour after it came up.
   230	
   231	Cael told them.
   232	
   233	He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
   234	
   235	Karis sat very still with the wallet on her knees.
   236	
   237	"My room," she said.
   238	
   239	"If you'll have it."
   240	
   241	"He asked for my room." She said it slowly, as if she were reading it off a page in a hand she did not know. "Out of every room on the hill." Then she looked up, and Cael saw that she was not frightened, or not only. "Yes. Tell him yes. Tell him I'll have the girl sent home early that day, and the kettle on, and every paper on the table turned over." She stopped. "No. Don't tell him the last part. I'll just do it."
   242	
   243	"I'll bring the kettle," said Brom.
   244	
   245	Lira was looking out at the rain. When she spoke it was to the glass.
   246	
   247	"Two years he's sat at the middle of the bench," she said. "Two years, and never once put anything of his on the table. Not a cup. Not a word he didn't have to." She turned round. "Good. That's all. Good."
   248	
   249	Brom stood and shouldered his kit bag. At the door he stopped, and put his hand flat to the left side of his coat, where the letter was, the one he had carried sealed since the forty-fifth day, and kept it there a moment, as if he were checking that the road had not already taken something.
   250	
   251	"The hour before light," he said, and went.
   252	
   253	Cael wrote it that night with the rain at last beginning to slacken against the glass.
   254	
