   129	It was a flat chain ferry, broad enough for one coach and its team and nothing else. A great chain ran from an iron ring sunk in the rock on the near side, down under the water, and up again to a winch-house on the far bank, and the ferry hauled itself along the chain by a wheel turned from its deck. The river came through the narrows deep and fast and brown and very loud. But it could not spread. There was no width for it to throw anything about in. It simply went through, as water goes through a pipe, and the ferry sat on top of it on its chain like a cat on a wall.
   130	
   131	The ferryman came out of his hut at the coach lamps with a lantern of his own. He was an old man, thin as a rake, in a coat that had been mended so often it was more mending than coat. He looked at the coach, and at the house's mark on its door, and at the river.
   132	
