   168	
   169	It was a deep straight cutting through a spur of the hill that came down to the river. Somebody a long time ago had decided to go through the spur rather than round it, and had dug a road down into the chalk and carted the chalk away, and the walls were still there, white and steep and streaked with green where the water ran. On the north side, uphill, the bank stood twenty feet over the road. On the south side, toward the river, it was lower, ten or twelve feet. Toward the far end of the cut, a stone's throw short of where it opened out again, the south bank had been eaten away underneath by some flood long ago, before the road had a ditch, so that its lip hung out over a hollow of shadow, like a brow over an eye.
