     1	# Chapter 48 — Two Hundred and Six
     2	
     3	The long room was full, and he counted it.
     4	
     5	It was the only use he could find for the quarter hour before the seat came in. The coach had gone down the bluff road at the usual pace behind Withrow's carriage, and the two coats at the road's foot had looked at it as they looked at it every Fifth-day, and the young officer at the side door had written the same two lines in his log. But the exchange's four steps had been crowded to the plinth this morning, and the benches behind Cael were filling with a noise he had not heard in that room since the preliminary. A closing, the house's counsel had said, was the one day besides the first when a proceeding sold.
     6	
     7	So he sat with lot seventeen under his left boot and his hands on the house's table, and counted the people, as he had once counted the words in an instrument.
     8	
     9	He took the benches first. There were forty of them, and for eleven sittings he had been able to count the heads on them at a glance. Today he had to go bench by bench, turning a little in his chair, and Bracken, beside him, watched him do it and said nothing, having very probably done it himself on the way in. Twenty-three had folding boards on their knees. There was a whole bench of district clerks in their grey, who had come because it was their seat and their Fifth-day. There were wool-factors, who owned the room and seemed to feel that it had been borrowed for long enough. An instructor in a blue-grey coat sat near the back with her arms folded, and he thought she was the lake house's. On the fourth bench sat Lira, Brom, Gault and the assistant with her inky collar, and at its far end, by the pillar, where a man could see the whole platform and both tables without turning his head, Seln sat with his coat buttoned.
    10	
    11	A hundred and eighty-seven on the benches.
    12	
    13	Then the people who were there by office. The seat's clerk at his small table. Ilsev in the middle chair below the platform, with her file at its angle, and her four district evaluators. The senior chair and the junior at the records table. At the petitioner's table Jent, and for the first time a clerk of his own, a thin young man with a satchel who looked as if he had been told not to touch anything. Three officers at the doors. At the house's table Withrow, the counsel, Karis, Bracken with the seal on his knees, and himself.
    14	
    15	And the hood, when it came.
    16	
    17	Nineteen. He added them twice, which was once more than the sum needed.
    18	
    19	Two hundred and six.
    20	
    21	He did not stop there, though he might have. He went down them again, more slowly, and this time he was not counting heads. He was doing what he had done on the night the instrument was served, when he had gone through nineteen leaves looking for the place where it lied. He had looked for that place in every sitting since. He had looked for it in Jent's weight in a chair, and in a seal that Havel had found on no register, and under a grey hood that had never once shown him a face.
    22	
    23	The clerks in their grey had come to see their own seat do its work. The correspondents would write down what was said, and get some of it wrong, and mean none of the errors. The four evaluators were working officers who had read every exhibit they were handed. Ilsev had asked the seat to minute the edge of her own chair, so that nobody could push her past it. At the records table sat a man who had receipted a hundred and seventy-nine leaves without losing one. And Jent, with his travelling inkwell and his mended cuffs, had given away every point that was not true as soon as he was shown it.
    24	
    25	There was nobody. He had known it for weeks, in the way he knew a figure he had not yet written down, and this morning, with the room full, he wrote it down.
    26	
    27	Two hundred and six people, and not one of them was dishonest.
    28	
    29	He did not know what he had expected a thing like this to look like. He had thought, when he was younger, that it would have a face somewhere, and that the face would look away. He looked along the benches at a hall of people who would all go home tonight and tell the truth about their day.
    30	
    31	"The seat," said the clerk, and two hundred and four of them stood up, and Cael stood with them.
    32	
