     1	# Chapter 50 — Within the Bell
     2	
     3	The rule was older than the wool exchange, and the house's counsel explained it on the First-day morning in the coach, with her bag on her knees and her steel rule lying across the bag.
     4	
     5	"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
     6	
     7	"Has a seat ever used it?" said Karis. "The recall."
     8	
     9	"In my time? Twice. Once to ask a guild master his mother's maiden name, which had been left off a form." The counsel's mouth moved at one corner. "Seats do not think of things late. They are very thorough about thinking of them early. But the rule stands, and we keep it, and in four days nobody will be able to say that the house was not where it was told to be."
