   349	"Let them escalate," said Lira. She had her feet to the fire and her chin on her knees. "They'll need better maps."
   350	
   351	"And better roads," said Brom.
   352	
   353	He said it feelingly, having carried a box up a ridge that morning.
   354	
   355	Cael looked at the two of them, and at a researcher with her pale fingers round a tin cup, and at a mild man in a dark coat who had sat at the edge of other people's tables for fifteen years.
   356	
   357	Karis wrote one word in the field archive.
   358	
   359	"Noted."
   360	
   361	Cael did not write it down. His book stayed shut. He put it into the satchel, in the pocket Hesk had cut for it from memory, and did up the buckle.
   362	
   363	The fire burned. Below the shelf a valley lay in the dark, with water going through it, and nobody had ever told him what it was called; beyond it there were seven ridges that he had counted, and more that he had not. Brom went back to the lip and sat down facing outward. After a little while Seln lay down under the pine, on his side, with the satchel under his head, and shut his eyes as a man does who has decided to trust a table.
   364	
   365	Cael sat on.
   366	
   367	He had been read at fourteen, in a room, in eleven seconds, and since that morning there had always been somebody deciding what he was. A warden had done it, and a provost, and a tournament office; a seat had done it under a hood, and a designation had done it that no office would own. Some of them had been kind, and most had been correct, and all of them had been writing in a column that he could not see.
   368	
   369	He listened for it now, on a shelf above a valley, as he had listened at a ring of churned turf for a threshold that was not there.
   370	
   371	There was the fire, and the water. Four people were breathing.
   372	
   373	And for the first time since his Kindling, nothing anywhere was deciding what he was, except him.

