editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:1:# Chapter 16 — The Column
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:The real errand had been the board, but he had a docket number to ask Bracken about, so he went back by the records hall. The counter's daybook lay open on the brass rail where Bracken had left it to dry, and Cael read the day's line before he could stop himself. It was a page of figures and he had never in his life been able to leave a page of figures unread. Four narrow columns had been ruled across it in the last few weeks, in Bracken's small capitals: *HOUSES. SPONSORS. FACULTIES. OTHER.* Under *OTHER*, yesterday, the figure was one hundred and forty-one. The heaviest line on the page, the Fifth-day before the sitting, said one hundred and ninety-six.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:161:Under his own name there was nothing to write. He drew a short line at the foot of each of the four columns, the way Karis had told him to, so that each could be added up; and under the first three he wrote a figure, the number of lines above it. Under his own he wrote *0*, and looked at it for a long time, and did not write anything beside it.
