editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:121:He was on one knee on the bottom step, threading a chain through the stair's rail. It was a long chain, the kind the porters used on the cart-shed doors, and he had already run it twice round the newel post. As the four of them came up he drew the last link through, set a padlock in it, and snapped the padlock shut. Then he stood up and hung a card on the chain, square in the middle, where it would be read by anybody who came to the stair. The card was in his square capitals. *PROGRAM FLOOR CLOSED TO OBSERVERS. BY ORDER.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:235:He thought of three sheets he had seen on Bracken's counter that morning before the coach went down. Each had Withrow's name at its foot and the ink still wet: releases of the house's answer, entire, to three faculties of registry law that had written to ask for it to teach from. Across the bottom of the top one, under her signature, she had added a line in her own hand, *For teaching. Let them argue.* Under that, smaller, in Bracken's upright capitals, someone had written *REGISTRAR'S RESERVATION NOTED*, and then, smaller still and in the same hand, *and overruled.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:1:# Chapter 22 — Both Roads
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:115:He had opened his eyes twice in the dairy, and the first time had been for the girl's chalk. *STAMPS STOP.* He knew that road. He knew, as a matter of his trade, what she did not: that a return like the one on the table had a route, office to office, and that the route ended where the stamps did. Past the last station there was no bag for it to go in, and no clerk to open it, and no drawer. A man could stand on the far side of that line and be watched by nobody whose watching went anywhere. He had never let himself think about it in those words before. He did not let himself go further now.
