editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:185:The lodge-man had painted it himself in white on black, years before Bracken came to the hill, and he went over the letters every spring with a fine brush. *CARTS — ROUND BY THE LANE. CALLERS — SIGN AND WAIT. POST — THE BAG ON THE HOOK. ANYTHING SEALED — FETCH THE REGISTRAR.* Cael watched Withrow find the last line with her eyes and stop on it. The lodge-man had done exactly what his board said. Anything sealed had come up the road, and he had sent his boy for the registrar, and he had shut his window so that he could stand in his own door and see the whole of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:1:# Chapter 28 — Where the Mark Sat
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:35:He went back across the court to the wing in the half dark. The lodge-man's window was open again. The rain had got at the board beside it in the night, and somebody had already chalked *ANYTHING SEALED* in fresh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:245:*NOBODY GOES IN AFTER HIM ALONE.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:1:# Chapter 30 — The Upper Ferry
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:121:Hesk's book was inside Cael's coat, against his ribs, where it had been every day since the gate. He could feel the corner of it when the coach rocked. He thought about the leaf at the front of it with four sets of initials, and *NOBODY GOES IN AFTER HIM ALONE* pressed so hard into the paper that you could read it from the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:1:# Chapter 56 — Doors
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:47:The notes were real, and that had surprised her a little. She had meant to sit a quarter of an hour, for the form of it, and say her sentence. Then the grey woman had begun on what a table could see and what it couldn't, and Lira had found the pencil in her hand, because it was all true and nobody had ever said it to her so plainly. *A panel sees half of you.* She had turned to the back of the carter's book, under *ME*, and taken it down as fast as she could write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:1:# Chapter 13 — The Hand
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:1:# Chapter 38 — Thresholds
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:337:She put the two flames out with a closing of her hand, and stood, and moved the line. She drew it again two feet further from him, and lit its ends again, and counted three and four, and the new thing asked, and he named it. She moved it two feet further and counted five and six, and it asked a little less surely, as a voice is less sure across a wider room. At ten feet he named it, and Karis wrote *10* in her notebook and drew a ring round it.
