editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:1:# Chapter 25 — The Rail
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:125:*For your file*, the steward's boy had said, handing it over after the finals, as if it were nothing; and nobody in her life had ever handed her anything *for your file* that she had wanted to keep. Her name and *Iron, Rank One, Wind*. His name, which she had decided she was allowed to know now, and *Silver, Rank Eight, Force*. Four lines of five filled. *Touch, confluence. Touch, Halcenvane. No touch. Touch, confluence. Two to one.* The panel's columns. *28.* *27.* And at the foot, pressed so hard into the paper that she could feel the letters from the back, Rooke's four words, which he had copied onto it at the inn without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:1:# Chapter 49 — Fifty-One Minutes
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:1:# Chapter 34 — A Good Room
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:5:The card was pinned at the board's head in Rooke's square capitals, as every card was. Under *LIRA* there were three lines, each with a date and a floor and a name. Rooke did not rub them out. He drew one stroke through each, level and unhurried, from the first letter to the last, the way a clerk strikes an entry he means to leave legible, and at the end of each line he wrote two words in the margin. Cael could not read them from the stair. He did not need to. He knew Rooke's two words for a bout not fought, because he had read them in that margin on other fighters' cards, and had never once expected to read them under hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:1:# Chapter 43 — The Glide
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:169:She turned the pages under the lantern. *FLOORS*, on the first leaf, in the capitals Karis hated. Sprung oak: six, and it gives you back more than you put in, so go early. Boards: five, and they're honest. Slate: four, and it takes the beat out of your knees by the third. New planks laid crossways: they slide, and nobody tells you. Chalk road in wind: four, and mind the ditch. She had underlined *ditch* twice and then, a week later, drawn a small rude face beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:*PEOPLE*, after that. A page for the Force: he builds on four, or on two when he's hurrying; you can see it in his knees. A page for the Silver at the confluence, with *go on your fifth* at the top in a box. A page for the Stone, which had almost nothing on it, because he never said anything, only a list of the places she had found him standing. Half a page for the Current girl. *Watch the front hip. Go along the bend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:177:And then, on the leaf at the back that she had headed *ME* and had not much liked writing on:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:1:# Chapter 41 — The Empty Hill
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:9:At the top the house was emptying: the outer court was full of carts, and the lodge-man stood at his window with the arrivals book turned to its other face, the departures, writing a name for every trunk that went under the arch. Third-years shouted to one another across the flags about the next term as if it were a country. Somebody had left a cracked practice lath leaning against the capped well with a paper on it that said *FREE*, and nobody had taken it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:1:# Chapter 59 — The Registry Line
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:*By charter of the registry, this house being chartered: TIER VERIFICATION available at the district station, Ostrand, four days west. No station lies east of the river crossing, and the station at the ford is unstaffed. TRAVEL ENDORSEMENTS may be lodged at this house for forward transmission; allow twenty days. ENROLLMENT INQUIRIES by the same. The chartered rates of this house are posted within. Complaints of a chartered house lie to the district seat.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:95:It was in pencil, in the broad margin beside *Silver*, and it ran half the way down the sheet. *Nets, 14. At 2 the net. Less 3 owed from the grey mare.* A line was ruled under it, and a total, and the total had been crossed out and corrected. Whoever had done it had wanted a piece of paper with room on it, and had found one.
