editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:153:"It was the only room on the sheet." Karis took a pencil from her notebook's spine and held it over the bare paper for a moment. Then she made a mark, small, and wrote beside it, in her exact hand, *ridge*. A finger's width on she made another. *Shelf; a pine; water at 36.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:167:Lira had her own book out by then, and had turned to the page with the capitals. *4 LOADED. 3 LIGHT. STAMPS STOP. 2 GO ON. 1 TURNS.* She had chalked that on a dairy wall once, and copied it out afterward, and carried it through eleven sittings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:1:# Chapter 52 — What Leaving Costs
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:191:"I've got it written. I did it on the stair in the records hall, watching him square copies, and it's under *ME*." She read it as she would have read a bout sheet, quickly, with her finger under the line. "The program. That's a hall painted for me, and a red book from a river academy, and a Stone on loan who writes two lines to my coach every week. A year of it. The trial rule, and twenty-seven on a card, and then twenty-seven under a hall's seal at the confluence, against a Silver who told me afterward to go on my fifth. Twenty-eight on the oak against a Blade. Four losses on my sheet, and I know what every one bought." She turned the leaf. "A sitting. The first after the year's close, behind a blue door, with my name fourth on the board. Rooke chalked it over the week's drills and ruled a line under it so nobody could wipe it off. He's been building toward that door since the recess before this year, and he never once asked me to promise I'd walk through it."
