editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:133:"That clause is why he's never stood in a station since Denvash," she said, to the heading and not to him. "It's never once asked a frame anything. It keeps him on a floor in front of people, where no Arbiter ever comes." She underlined the heading. "Every paper they've sent up this hill has broken itself on that clause. This one hands it back to us whole and asks for one thing only. A station. The provision doesn't interest them at all. They're trying to move him back inside the instrument."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:137:"I don't know why that distinction feels important. Noting it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:87:It was a small thing. Her pen came down an inch over the paper and stayed there. She looked across at the instrument lying open on the table's far side, at its first leaf, and he knew what she was looking at, because he had looked at it himself all night. *To the Chancellor, Halcenvane Academy, and to the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, unclassified.*
