editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:143:*7. The petitioner notes, and adopts as its own, the advisory entered in the prior year by an evaluator of the Compact upon the fielding clause of the tournament charter, which reads in full: "The clause says what it says. The enrollment is lawful on its face and adjudicated at public record. If the Compact wishes practitioners of this kind not to compete, it should write different rules — not misread the ones it has."*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:79:"*What evidence, if any, would defeat the petition?*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:181:"*They're trying to move him back inside the instrument.*" She moved her finger down a line. "*I don't know why that distinction feels important. Noting it.*" She laid the open notebook on the cloth beside the slips. "Cael put a question mark beside it in his notebook. I saw him do it. I've owed him the answer since the first night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:67:"At the fourth sitting I was allowed one question," she said. "I asked the petitioner what evidence, if any, would defeat the petition. His answer is in the minute, and I have read it most days since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:103:"The Compact's senior evaluator advised upon the question in three sentences, and the petition quotes them, with approval, at its seventh paragraph. I did not put them there. They say that the enrollment is lawful upon its face, and that if the Compact wishes practitioners of this kind not to compete it should write different rules. *Practitioners of this kind.* Nobody writes that of a man he expects a frame to read."

