editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Every person who has ever decided a thing about me had a face, and I could read it. Pellin at Denvash, with her brass plate. Coss across a desk. Vastin with his frames. The provost who was frightened and couldn't hide it. Umber polishing his spectacles over a sheet he couldn't score. I read every one of them from across a room, and every reading was worth something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:241:"Here's the other half," said Lira. "There was no door out of that hall but the one they pointed at. I went out of it with the form in my pocket, and for a year after that I knocked on guild doors. I asked six of them for a letter and walked to two. One wouldn't let me in the door. The other let me into the hall, and read the form, and was very polite." She turned her head toward him. "Then I came to Ardenmere with nothing left to knock on, and one evening I heard a noise like somebody hitting a boiler with a chain, three streets off, and went to look. Vell's yard. Nobody sent me there. It wasn't on any form. It was round the back of everything, where the town kept what it didn't want to look at, and that turned out to include me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:75:It was the Greyvane ruling. She had laid it down before anything else, before the paper she was answering had a single paragraph answered, on the bare table in the middle of three cleared feet of wood. The ruling, the certified transcript under it, and Prynn's own copy of the provision on top of both, in that small exact archivist's hand Cael had first seen on a high desk in a room that smelled of cold paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:91:Nobody had asked him that about the name before. Not at Denvash, where Pellin had stamped it on his card; not at Greyvane, where it had been read out over his head; not on any form since. Once, at Ardenmere, he had asked Vell to put it in her book the right way round, which was the nearest he had come. The registry gave him the name the way it gave a ledger a number. People said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:105:The first was Karis's own finding from Greyvane, the long one, the one she had built from the standardization directive's repeal schedules a line at a time until she could say, flatly, in front of a seat, that nobody had ever repealed the clause. It had been left behind, like a child at a fair, when everything round it was swept up and carried off. The second was the house counsel's reconstruction, made the year after, by another road, through the registry's own index of surviving provisions, without reading a word of Karis's first. The third was Prynn's copy and the note on its cover, from the archive at Greyvane, which showed the clause standing in the founding text with nothing written against it in two hundred years of hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:207:He stood by the table with the old Log under his arm. She had not been testing him. She had used him, the way she used the code's third volume and Prynn's copy and the sheaf with no note on it: as the best source she had on one question. It was the most useful he had ever been to her, and he did not mind the method in the least.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:25:"Umber," he had said. "At Norhold. The man who kept the instrument. It couldn't score me, and he had a whole office behind him that would have liked a number, and he wrote *no number goes here* instead."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:125:The man from Rhagen who wrote things down said something courteous. Cael could not hear it, but he could see the shape of it: a visiting officer's surprise, a question about the house's custom, a slight lift of the board to show that he had signed the lodge book like everybody else. The man who did not write said nothing. The lake house officer looked at the card and then at her three pencils. The old man in the fur collar had brought his stool, and he set it down on the stones and sat on it, as if to see what would happen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:127:Rooke answered with one word. Cael did not hear that either, but Ephram, who had come out of wing three behind the visitors and was close enough, told him afterward what it was. It was *No.* The Rhagen man had then asked, very politely, by whose order, since the card said *by order*, and Rooke had looked at him for a while and said, "Mine," and that had been the second word. Then Rooke turned his back on them and went in at the hall's side door, and a moment later the shutters began to close along the gallery's high windows, one after another, all down the north wall, from the inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:Gault had added the three cards in his head, and again on the back of his hand. *Twenty-two and two-thirds. Entered.* The clerk had written it in the wing's book. Up in the gallery four pencils had written it too, and Cael had heard them. Ephram had come down the room from the east rail with the soft-folded chart and entered it below the Blade, in his cramped square hand, *gave her the first, bad arm*, and had not drawn a question mark beside it. Then he had looked up past Cael's shoulder at the man from Rhagen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:241:*Gault's minute for yesterday says the demonstration was conducted as on every other day. Umber's note says nothing is to be added in his name. Withrow wrote "Let them argue" across the bottom of a release. Three people, three lines, none of them meant for this year. Somebody is going to open those minutes long after the seat has ruled and the weeklies have stopped printing, and find that every honest person in the room left one sentence there for them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:59:"That's what one of them asked the clerk. On the east hall's gallery. Friendly, the way you'd ask about the weather." She threw her apple core out over the wall into the scrub, a long throw. "The clerk told Rooke. Rooke's had her keeping a second ledger since a little after the first sitting. Every name on the gallery, every morning, and their house, and how long they stay. He reads it every night when he locks up. He never said why." She held up four fingers, folded down one at a time. "Rhagen, Rhagen, the lake house, and a place up north she had to ask him to spell. Twenty-six names in a fortnight, and those four are the only ones with no business with us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:169:The trouble was the strangers. Three cards and a clerk who knew him were one kind of audience. Four officers from four houses, with the broadsheets and the tournament books in their satchels, were another. Rhagen wrote down every exchange of every bout it could see and bound the result and sold it; somebody in an inn that very night would be laying tomorrow's figure beside last season's, line for line. Whatever he did must look exactly like the record, which meant like a strong Iron with a few odd gifts on a good day. And it must not look like the last bout, or the one before, because a record that matches itself has been arranged, and men whose trade is arrangements can smell it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:13:"Greyvane," he said. "Prynn, still?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:283:The one to Vell was shorter. She had kept a ledger that rated him before any registry did, and he had written to her at the close of each year. He told her that the year had closed, and that he had been found lawful and revoked in one paragraph. He wrote that he did not think her ledger had ever been wrong about him, and that he was now somewhere it could not be checked.

