editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1260:- **Jent:** "Chancellor."; "Advocate."; "Hesk-ward."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1279:- (a) **Leave is ruled at the first closing** and the closing delivered at the second, so that Karis has a week knowing she has the room, and Jent's ten minutes and her fifty-one fall on different days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/STATE_LEDGER.md:1299:- **The count of the room (day 187):** 187 on the benches, 19 by office, 206 with Cael. Jent has a clerk (thin, young, unnamed).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-003.md:25:- **Preliminary**: lot seventeen; **Ilsev empanelled**; Jent conceding and striking his own overstatement; the counsel's four movements; the ruling — both things at once; Ilsev's scope entered in her words. Coach: "And it's proceeding." **"…started counting doors."**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:41:He knew what was in the case on Karis's shelf, and that this room ran on it. The frame they meant to stand him in was the thing on the leaf, appended to the reading and not the reading, and every person on that platform would go home tonight not knowing it, and so would Jent, perhaps, and so would the clerk. He was the only one in the long room, besides Karis at his elbow, who knew what the argument was about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:47:Jent stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:59:"It follows," said Jent, "that the second schedule to the petition is wrong in one particular. It suggests, at its ninth line, that the first renewal was sealed out of its order. It was not. It was sealed nine days after the panel sat, because an officer of an inspecting delegation held the registry's copy for his countersignature, as he was entitled to do, and the respondent's advocate has shown the seat the very leaf." He took one sheet from the folder after all, and held it out to the clerk. "The petitioner withdraws that line, and tenders the schedule again without it. I should not like the seat to rule upon a schedule with a mistake in it. Least of all one of mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:65:"And lastly," said Jent. "A great deal was said at the preliminary about the evaluator of record at Halcenvane. I said some of it. I should like it in this minute, in plain words, that the petitioner accepts that officer as competent in every particular, accepts his panels as properly held, and accepts every figure he has signed as honestly come by." He let his hands fall. "The petition relies upon none of these matters. It never has. I mention them only so that nobody is put to the trouble of proving what I do not dispute."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:75:It was as clean a win as anything in the whole proceeding, and it weighed nothing, because Jent had not needed the ground he was giving. He was a man clearing a table before a meal. Every concession left the petition smaller and harder, and nearer the single thing it had always been: a question for the seat's discretion, about a frame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:97:Jent overtook them on the steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:99:The rain had thinned to a mist, and the market street ran shining down toward the river with the carts standing in it. Withrow had gone ahead to her carriage and bowed to nobody, and Bracken was at the coach door with the seal. Jent came down the four steps behind the house's party with his one folder under his arm, and at the bottom he stopped beside Karis and waited until she had to look at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:22:- **The closings**: two hundred and six people, none dishonest; Jent's ten minutes; **Karis's fifty-one-minute closing** under the leave the counsel obtained (§11 #4) — the best argument the series stages, and it changes nothing; Jent: "the registry does not dispute a syllable of it"; **the declined certification entered as an absence** and four pens stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:32:- Jent calls Withrow **Chancellor**; the "twenty-six years" line belongs to the counsel; Withrow's tenure unnumbered; Vastin has no stated age (OWNER #17; §11 #13) [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: replaces "fifty-two"; audit A6].
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:22:- **The merits sittings of the term after the recess** [B5-reconciled 2026-10-05: season word struck; audit A1] (two or three on the page, briefly): Jent conceding more; production orders; Karis's supplemental submissions; the counsel and Karis preparing the fossil-provision leave for the closing (BOOK_MAP §11 #4). **Havel's window** (~2k): exhibits logged; he rereads six entries; he does not write a seventh. **Vastin's window** (~1k): the proceedings' request for certification renewed; he declines in one line; nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:13:Above it, the line he had been half expecting for a fortnight. *Appearing for the petitioner as procurator of record: Jent.* He had read that name once before, at the foot of the instrument's last leaf, under the attestation, and Bracken had not known it then. Cael looked at the registrar now, and Bracken gave the smallest shake of the head: still nobody he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:*Per seal. Jent, the name off the last leaf. Seventeen days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:95:"Advocate," said Jent, and it was the third thing he said in that room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:101:Jent nodded to the three behind her as the house's attendance, and to Cael as a man nods to a party in a matter, and went back to his chair and unscrewed the inkwell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:107:The house's ran to a hundred and seventy-three items, in Karis's smallest hand. The counsel slid it over with one finger. Jent picked it up, weighed it a moment in his palm the way a man weighs a letter for its postage, opened it at the index leaf and read that, all of it, with his spectacles on. Then he initialled the receipt at the foot and passed it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:117:"I'd ask that the respondent's answer go into the record whole," said Jent. "Not the summary. If the respondent has no objection."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:133:And last the thing Cael trusted most: the little space before an answer that cost something, when a person chose how much of it to give. Every official he had ever sat across from had one. Jent did not. Question and answer followed each other with nothing between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:151:Four questions. One hard, Cael thought, one sly, two easy, and the counsel had put the sly one second, where a man tired of easy ones might trip. Cael had been counting the clock over the hearth between each question and its answer, and he had the four counts in his head: none, none, none, none. The sly one had cost Jent exactly what *No* had cost him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:155:When the clerk asked whether counsel had anything further, Jent was already screwing the lid onto the inkwell, a turn and a quarter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:159:The clerk's pen stopped. Jent looked down the table at Cael and inclined his head exactly as far as a procurator inclines it to a party.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:201:*Four nothings. I have only ever found a man by what he was afraid to lose, and Jent brought nothing into that room that was his to lose. The compliment he paid Karis the way you pay a fee at a counter, the right amount, honestly. Our whole answer he handed back across the table like a parcel addressed to somebody else.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:81:"Jent," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:Cael walked up the strip of floor between the two parties' tables to the house's place, and the old trade went under his boots as he went. The wool-factors had carved their lot numbers into the planking a hundred years ago, row after row, so that every bale had its place and every buyer could find it, and the registry had planed and waxed the boards and set its furniture down on the numbers without troubling to hide them. *Nine*, *ten*, *eleven*. Across the strip Jent was already at the petitioner's table with his inkwell unscrewed; he rose when Withrow came in, to his measured height, and sat again. Cael sat down at the house's table with *seventeen* under his left foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:*The seat opened at the second bell.* The clerk read the designation; Havel wrote the minute it ended. *The petitioner's procurator entered the petition.* Jent stood at his table with his leaf in one hand, read the petition's working paragraphs in an even voice that gave no one of them more weight than another, named his nine exhibits and sat; Havel wrote the minute he stood and the minute he sat. Nine minutes. *The respondent's counsel entered the answer.* She rose, small and grey with a steel rule in her hand, and summarized eleven pounds in a voice that did not hurry and did not linger; twenty-three minutes. Havel took the receipts for both lists of exhibits in the receipt book, nine items and a hundred and seventy-three, and wrote the numbers, and initialled them.

