editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:85:*Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:"This was on my counter," he said, "when I came back from the press with the rulings. Stamped. Countersigned. In order." He read from it. "*Research standing: floor and archive hours, by application. Applicant: K. Dellenmoor. Hours: the records hall, this night, from the eighth bell until the work is done. Purpose: the house's answer to an instrument served this day. Received and entered.*" He lowered it. "The stamp is the assessment wing's. The hand on the received line is the wing's. The form was built in the recess for an office that had never had a use for it until this morning." He looked down at the three of them on his steps. "Somebody has been expecting us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:107:The card was still in its brass holder, in Bracken's small upright hand, as it had hung all last year. *Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.* What was new sat under the card: a brass lock with a heavy plate, let into the old oak by somebody who knew wood. Its key hung on a cord round her neck. The cord was too long, and the key had swung out over the top of the books.
