+- Ch19: clean. The five-bean council is exceptionally clear by voice and physical reference. No collision among Brom, Lira, Karis and Seln.
+- Ch20: clean. The third sitting's quoted arguments and ruling remain distinct from listener-facing paraphrase. The figures 44 and 54 are deliberately written numerals on Cael's private note, then explained as days.
+- Ch21: clean. Lira's ferry research, board notice, and five-person dairy council remain attributable. The slate numerals are concise field notes and are unpacked in speech.
+- Ch22: clean. The five-person recollection gives each prior speaker an explicit name or action. `bow` in `key worn bright at the bow` = BOH. Seln's italic return flattens as a deliberate series of form sentences.
+- Ch23: clean. Bout card, manifest acceptance, floor notes and bread demonstration are all intelligible. No ambiguous speaker run.
+- Ch24: clean. Scores, counts and floor measurements are spoken in prose except for Cael's compact notebook line; the latter is introduced as writing and its `SW` is a compass abbreviation in a visual note, not intended dialogue.
+- Ch25: clean. The order, steward's sheet and scores are introduced before their figures. Lira/Cael and Seln/council sequences retain clear ownership.
+
+### Ch26–35
+
+- Ch26: clean. The preliminary challenge reading, gate order and recurring “one question” are all separated clearly from ordinary dialogue.
+- Ch27: clean. The cache council and Anchor naming remain attributable across all five voices; no cut creates a speaker collision.
+- Ch28: clean. Paper, watermark and favour evidence is clear by ear. `wound the counter clock` = WOWND.
+- Ch29: clean. The catechism/cache sequence has no broken join or ambiguous exchange.
+- Ch30: clean. Havel’s registers, the upper ferry scene and the beginning of the ambush read continuously. `grey of the lead` = LED (pencil material).
+- Ch31: clean. Combat positions preserve speaker and actor identity. `lead` as team leader = LEED; `read her` = RED; drawn `bow` = BOH.
+- Ch32: clean. The Anchor FRAGMENT ACQUIRED notice is complete in its fenced block and will flatten line by line. In `the read lay where he had left it`, `read` is the noun/ability and should be REED.
+- Ch33: clean. Doorway and idle naming sequences hold. A second blank line before line 275 is cosmetic and narrator-silent.
+- Ch34: clean. Birthday and inventory material has no ear or join fault.
+- Ch35: clean. The quoted letter runs across four paragraphs using standard multi-paragraph quotation: opening marks on each paragraph and the closing mark at line 265.
+
+### Ch36–45
+
+- Ch36: clean. The cache leaf and its handling remain clear in document and scene voice.
+- Ch37: clean. The full telling and first stability warning are intact. The Shadow FRAGMENT notice is complete in its fenced block and flattens intelligibly.
+- Ch38: clean. Threshold and door experiments are easy to track. The notebook `10` is a measurement record, introduced as writing.
+- Ch39: clean. The four findings and the stated limit are coherent and attributable.
+- Ch40: clean. Vastin’s borrowed-room scene has no speech-ownership or name collision.
+- Ch41: clean. Hesk’s letter and the council conflict remain distinct. `wound it` (the clock) = WOWND.
+- Ch42: clean. Road teaching and the night-road sequence retain actor and speaker positions.
+- Ch43: clean. Lira’s Current bout and personal book are unambiguous; no post-cut join fault.
+- Ch44: clean. The sixth sitting, Havel material and Withrow exchange are coherent by ear.
+- Ch45: clean. Brom’s frame, Lira versus the Blade, and Lira learning Cael’s full rate remain clearly staged.
+
+### Ch46–51
+
+- Ch46: clean. Gault’s notes and the Archmarshal’s certification refusal remain distinct instruments. The year-end Mire bout and numerical figure echo correctly.
+- Ch47: clean. The apparent fragment `In the records hall` is an intentionally begun Log entry; the next paragraph explicitly strikes it through and replaces it with `Not mine to enter.`
+- Ch48: clean. The closing arguments, mock-seat rehearsal and speaker shifts remain clear throughout.
+- Ch49: clean. The protected certification-minute wording is left exactly as the form gives it. The immediately following narration expressly distinguishes the Archmarshal’s refusal from Gault’s evaluator-of-record title, satisfying OWNER-DECISIONS #47 without touching the protected line.
+- Ch50: clean. Call-day exposition, the district station and Ilsev’s return keep quoted record text distinct from speech. No tag or join fault.
+- Ch51: clean. The ruling is a correct two-paragraph quotation: line 177 intentionally remains open and line 179 opens and closes the final quoted paragraph. The panel, ruling and records-hall aftermath remain attributable.
+
+### Ch52–60
+
+- Ch52: clean. The three-road pricing council keeps all five speakers distinct. Karis’s forfeiture bibliography is a correctly marked sequence of multi-paragraph quotation, including the narrated pauses and resumed quoted heads. The protected exchange at line 163 remains intact.
+- Ch53: clean. Withrow’s concealed five-day arithmetic, the protected ninth-fragment rule, Lira’s hall leave-taking and the Crown-yard opening remain coherent. No speaker or action join is broken.
+- Ch54: clean. The Crown-yard exchanges, Blade FRAGMENT ACQUIRED notice and post-bout inventory all read continuously. The fenced notice is complete and protected.
+- Ch55: clean. Four withdrawals, Karis’s lapsed heads, Gault’s final note and the assistant’s numbered farewell are distinct. `wound` the counter clock = WOWND; the key’s `bow` = BOH.
+- Ch56: clean. Lira’s open-door seminar, Seln’s wing departure, the three-box loading and the district officer’s returned notice are narratable without ambiguity.
+- Ch57: clean. Seln’s absent-desk record, the service’s later audit, the burning of duplicate returns and the crossroads fire remain clearly separated in time and voice. Key `bows` = BOHZ.
+- Ch58: clean. The future administrative close is deliberately presented before the narrative returns to First-day; the transition explicitly says so. Road speech and ford actions remain identifiable.
+- Ch59: clean. The last waystation, abandoned station, register, threshold beyond the station bound, rider’s turn, post-rider and ridge climb all hold. No road dialogue loses its owner. The map and line imagery do not create a spoken-name collision.
+- Ch60: clean. The coordinator-directed continuity fixes all land: Seln is explicitly kept awake until the rota argument and later lies down under the pine; his surveyor’s case is placed with the boxes and covered by the canvas; his pooled contribution is identified before Brom’s question about what can be bought. The final fire dialogue and inventory are clear.
+
+## Full-read provisional judgment
+
+- No quotation mismatch, unclear speaker, broken join, or protected-text fault found in the 60-chapter read.
+- No narrative ear collision requires a line change. Homographs are resolvable in context; pronunciation directions should cover the few high-risk cases.
+- One narrator-format candidate remains: ch12’s all-caps program card `LIRA / FORCE THIRD-YEAR`, because `fpaudio.py` does not transform `/` and the synthesis layer may voice it literally. Verify uniqueness and choose a spoken-form replacement outside protected text.
+
+## Closing mechanical audit
+
+- Paragraph parity: 8 paragraphs have an odd count of straight quotation marks. All are valid multi-paragraph quotations: ch35:259, 261, 263 close at 265; ch51:177 closes in the following quoted paragraph at 179; ch52:215, 217, 219, 221 resolve through the continued bibliography and its narrated resumptions. No odd-asterisk paragraph; no curly quote.
+- Scene breaks: 254; all have a blank line before and after. Fences: 3 complete blocks / 6 fence lines. No stray backtick, underscore or strikethrough marker.
+- Whitespace: one pre-existing trailing space, ch17:185, already in Lane A’s report; narrator-silent. No tab or doubled space.
+- Paragraph ends: all apparent exceptions are introductions to the next displayed line, fence contents, or deliberate written fragments. The two high-risk apparent fragments are intentional: ch14’s struck draft and ch47’s begun-and-struck Log entry.
+- Repeated-word scan: only grammatical `had had` (18 matches). No doubled article/preposition found.
+- Numerals: the remaining non-heading numerals are document, notebook, board or tally marks (petition paragraphs 34 and 7; Priority Level 4; ch16’s visual `0`; days 44/54/45; the five road figures; ratings 28/27/26; notebook measurements 10 and 36; Day 88). Each is introduced as written material or immediately glossed. No T-number or unexplained technical abbreviation.
+- Slash: exactly one in all manuscript text, on ch12’s program card. Proposed required spoken-form fix: `LIRA / FORCE THIRD-YEAR` → `LIRA AGAINST FORCE THIRD-YEAR`.
+- Exact-match check: the complete old card string occurs in exactly one manuscript file/line; the complete new card string occurs nowhere. The old text is not in `protected-patterns.txt` or BOOK_MAP §8.
+- Protected-pattern check: all 51 regex patterns are present. Forty-six occur once. Five occur twice by deliberate callback/quotation (`distinction feels important`, the Compact advisory, the challenge address, Karis’s question, and `move him back inside the instrument`). All three FRAGMENT ACQUIRED blocks match their protected notice wording.
+- Long-paragraph sweep: 106 flattened paragraphs exceed 700 characters. Longest: ch1:119, 1,205 characters. These are inventory-only findings because `fpaudio.py` splits at sentence boundaries.
+- Homographs: `lead` is LED for pencil/seal material (ch30, ch58, ch59) and LEED for the verb/front/leader; every `wound` is WOWND (tape, scarf or clock); `tear` is TAIR; all `live` occurrences are LIV; `bow` is BOH for a drawn bow and key bow, BAU for the courtesy; `present` is always PREZ-ent; `read` is REED as Cael’s noun and RED as past tense. Context is sufficient, but these belong in the direction notes.
+- Bare `as`: 1,313 tokens total; 451 comma-`as` and 428 `as he/she/it/they/we/you/I` constructions. Read in place; manner, comparison, habit and true simultaneity are clear. No line needs conversion to `while`.
+- Protected and requested joins: ch13:217–219, ch18:97, ch49’s certification minute and ch60’s case/fire/purse repairs all pass the second-ear read.

