editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:57:"*Asset-restriction.* Section fourteen, part nine. *The registry may restrict, freeze, or hold in abeyance any standing, record, sponsorship, endorsement, travel credential, or institutional relationship attaching to an unclassified person, pending resolution of practitioner status.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:73:"The morning after it's entered, a practitioner goes down to the ferry as he goes every week, and holds out his pass, and the ferryman has had a letter. A short one, very polite, in the plainest form. The pass is held in abeyance. The ferryman is sorry. The ferryman has a licence of his own and a family to feed." Her voice did not rise at all. "So he goes up to his guild hall, where they've drunk with him for twenty years, and they've had a letter too. It tells them their association with him is *under restriction*, and the master reads those two words and understands that his own name goes on the next letter if he so much as nods to the man in the street. So he goes to the records office to see his own file and argue with it, and the clerk tells him it's sealed. Kept, every page, perfectly safe, and nobody may open it. Not he. Not anyone he pays. Not anyone who might stand up somewhere and say what's in it on his behalf." She paused. "And every house that ever put its name beside his gets a letter. This house would get one. After that, every question anybody asked about him would reach us already answered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:55:"Put it back." He folded the spectacles and held them in one hand. "If the seat goes to my filing, which it may, and finds one spelling there and another in yours, it's one more thing that doesn't match. And it was my mistake. I made it at this counter in my fourth year with the frame's report in one hand and the pen in the other." He looked at her. "I'd sooner the record had it the way I wrote it. Mark it as mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:45:"May I ask you something?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:47:"You may ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:149:On the first count she stepped. On the second she put her weight on her front foot, the long lie, and her front foot came down inside the lane, and the lane took it. It slid her front foot a hand's breadth south, sideways, out from under her, and her whole lie went wrong with it. She did not fall. She caught herself on the back foot with a grunt and stood there off balance in the painted floor's dust, and the four-count was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:159:"Three strides," said Lira. "Short." She was breathing hard. "But I didn't fall. Again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:121:"Then we're agreed." He took off his spectacles and let them hang on their cord, and turned over a leaf of his own notes. "Your renewals table, Advocate. Two dates to every row, and the gap between them accounted for. I've not seen that before. Somebody was in the room." He let the leaf fall back. "The best-documented enrollment I've reviewed in thirty years. Genuinely."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:69:*The challenge does not allege fraud in the enrollment's execution, nor contest the text of the provision under which it was executed. It asserts that an unclassifiable practitioner's presence within a chartered institution's evaluation architecture presents an evaluation-integrity question which the demonstration provision was not drafted to answer and cannot answer, and petitions the registry for compelled re-evaluation of the enrollee under standard Arbiter protocol, the enrollment to stand or fall upon that evaluation's result.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:105:He told her the first page. He had it nearly whole; he had been listening the way he listened to anything that would have to be written down later. *Does not allege fraud. Nor contest the text.* *Evaluation integrity.* *Compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol.* *Stand or fall.* She listened to all of it without interrupting, with her pen still in her hand, and when he had finished she looked past him, down the narrow stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:125:"Paragraph six." The rule went under it. "*The provision by which the enrollment was made is acknowledged to be valid, and to have survived in law the standardization directive. Neither is put in issue.*" She lifted the rule. "I came up this hill expecting to spend the year proving exactly that. Whether the clause is still law. Whether the directive killed it. Whether a house may still enroll by demonstration on a clause out of the founding text. They've signed it for me, under their own seal, in the sixth paragraph of their first paper." She set the rule on the leaf. "Nobody has ever done that to me before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:209:"We went to Norhold," said Withrow, "and came home third, and you have all heard me say so, and you will not hear me say it again." She let a breath go by. "Here is the year instead. It is quieter work and it is harder work. Third is a figure now, written down in other people's books, and a figure in other people's books is a thing other people may try to rub out. There is one way to keep it, and it is the plain way. Say what you are. Be it. Leave it where anyone can check." Her eyes went nowhere in particular, which with Withrow was always somewhere. "Somebody will be checking. Somebody always is, now. Good. The research standings post on the covered walk this morning. The program's floor hours post at noon. Go to your assignments."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:7:He was a big man, though not as big as Brom. He was thick through the hips and short in the neck, with grey coming in at the temples and forearms that had spent a life on wet rope. He wore a plain shirt and the guild's dark sash knotted at the hip, and his feet were bare. Somebody on the south benches called his name, and somebody else, and then the whole south side of the hall took it up, a deep warm roar with a laugh in it, the sound of a town shouting for one of its own. He did not look up at it. He lifted one hand off his thigh a finger's breadth and let it fall again, and the roar went up twice as loud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:He took the rail at the north end, as he had on the second morning of the year. The high windows were behind him. At this hour the sun came through them low, and laid long bars of light across the painted floor below, over the white crosses and the arrows, so that the whole hall looked ruled like a page in a ledger, with a line for every stride a fighter might take. Lira said she could read it. She said that on a bright morning she could see her own feet a quarter-beat before they landed, because the light told her where the shadow of her heel would fall. She had said it lightly, the way she said most things that mattered to her, and then she had gone out of her way never to train in the afternoon again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:35:"*An offer was made by the lead rider to the enrollee,*" said Bracken. "*In these words.*" He gave them exactly. Cael could have given them himself. "*The order is addressed to two parties. The second may come with us of his own accord. If he comes voluntarily, nobody here need refuse anybody anything, and we can all be gone before the third bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:"*Anything sealed, fetch the registrar,*" she read aloud. "You did, and you shut your window, and you stood where you could see the whole of it." She nodded to him, the nod she gave her senior staff. "Your board did very well this morning. Put a fresh coat on it in the spring, if you would. I should like it to last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:81:"You may hold that," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:145:"It doesn't say *shall*," the girl said. "Or *upon cause*. It says the office *may*, at its discretion. There isn't any condition at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:151:Everything they had spent six weeks fighting about was gone. Whether the clause was law; whether the house's record was honest; whether anybody had done anything wrong. Jent had handed all of it across the floor in his first ten minutes, and it could never be taken back now, because it was in the minute in his own voice. What was left was one question with no paper in it at all: may the registry stand him in a frame? And everyone in the room knew already what the frame would say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:167:At the first turn the four district evaluators stopped looking at nothing and began to look at her. At the second, the front benches put their pencils down. At the third, the hood spoke, for the only time that day. At the fourth the rule lay pointing at the platform again, as it had when she rose, and the gallery made the long low sound galleries make when they may not clap.
